var e={"tech:punchcards":{intro:`Punched-card tabulating stores information as holes in stiff paper cards: one card for each person, sale or part. Electric machines sort the cards, count them and add up the numbers punched in each column. It is how governments, railways, insurers and large companies keep their records and do their sums.`,how:`A clerk at a keypunch cuts holes into the card's columns; the row a hole sits in stands for a digit or a letter. A sorter drops cards into pockets by the hole in one chosen column, hundreds a minute. The tabulator feels for holes with metal brushes, and each hole lets a current through to advance a counter. Wires plugged into a board tell it which columns to add and what to print.`,history:`Herman Hollerith's electric tabulators counted the 1890 US census, finishing the basic population count far faster than the hand count of 1880 had. In 1896 Hollerith founded the Tabulating Machine Company to sell and rent his machines. In 1911 it merged into the Computing-Tabulating-Recording Company, which renamed itself International Business Machines, IBM, in 1924. In 1928 IBM brought out its 80-column card with narrow rectangular holes, holding nearly twice as much as the old round-holed 45-column card. In 1936 the new US Social Security scheme began keeping the earnings records of millions of workers on IBM cards and machines, the largest bookkeeping job yet attempted.`,facts:[`The 1890 US census tabulated about 63 million people on Hollerith machines.`,`IBM's 1928 card held 80 characters, each column with 12 punching positions.`,`In 1924 the Computing-Tabulating-Recording Company became International Business Machines.`]},"tech:bizdp":{intro:`Business data processing means giving an electronic computer the routine paperwork of an office: valuing stock, costing products, working out orders, invoices and wages. Instead of rooms of clerks with calculators and punched-card machines, one machine reads in the figures, does the sums and prints the results. The first company to try it is not a bank or an insurer but a British catering firm.`,how:`Records are held on punched cards, paper tape or magnetic tape and processed in batches: a whole day's orders are read in, matched against a master file in the same order, and new totals and printed documents come out. Programs are written by hand in machine code. The work is simple arithmetic in enormous quantities, so fast input and printing matter more than raw calculating speed.`,history:`In 1947 two managers of J Lyons and Co, the London tea-shop and bakery chain, toured the US and came home convinced that a computer could run their office. In 1949, having helped to fund the EDSAC computer at Cambridge University, Lyons began building its own machine on the same design, LEO I, the Lyons Electronic Office, with John Pinkerton as chief engineer. In 1951 LEO I began its first regular job, valuing the output of the bakeries, the first routine office work done by a computer anywhere. In 1951 the US Census Bureau also accepted the first UNIVAC I. In 1954 Lyons set up LEO Computers Ltd to sell machines to other firms. By 1964 IBM's 1401, launched in 1959 for business use, was the most common computer in the world.`,facts:[`LEO I's first regular job, Bakery Valuations, ran from September 1951.`,`In 1959 an IBM 1401 business system rented from about $2,500 a month.`]},"tech:payroll":{intro:`Computer payroll hands one of the biggest and dullest jobs in any company to a machine: working out each employee's pay from hours, rates, overtime, tax and deductions, then printing the pay slips and cheques. It is the first job many businesses give their first computer, because it comes round every week and every mistake angers somebody.`,how:`Each employee has a master record on cards or tape: name, rate of pay, tax code and the totals so far this year. Each week the hours from the clock cards are punched in, sorted into the same order and run past the master file. The program works out gross pay, takes off tax and other deductions, updates the year's totals and prints the slips. Clerks check only the cases the machine flags as odd.`,history:`In 1949 Henry Taub started Automatic Payrolls in New Jersey with his brother Joe, doing other firms' wages with bookkeeping machines and a lot of hand work. In 1954 LEO I took over the payroll of Lyons' own staff in London. In 1954 General Electric's Appliance Park in Louisville, Kentucky, began running its payroll on a UNIVAC I, among the first US companies to buy a computer for business work. In 1961 the New Jersey firm, by then named Automatic Data Processing, went public with about 300 clients. By 1962 it was doing its customers' payrolls on an IBM 1401 computer.`,facts:[`Automatic Payrolls began in 1949 with one client and about $6,000 of capital.`,`When it went public in 1961, ADP had about 125 staff and $400,000 a year in revenue.`]},"tech:reservations":{intro:`A real-time transaction system lets hundreds of clerks in different cities look up and change one shared set of records at the same moment, with answers in seconds. Airline booking is the first big use: every agent sees the true number of seats left on every flight, and a seat sold in Chicago is gone in New York at once.`,how:`Agents' desk terminals are linked by leased telephone lines to a central computer that keeps the seat inventory and passenger records on fast drums and discs. Each request is a short transaction: find the flight, hold a seat, record the passenger's name and phone number. Changes to a record are made one at a time so two agents cannot sell the last seat twice, and a second computer stands ready to take over if the first fails.`,history:`In 1953 American Airlines' president, C R Smith, sat next to an IBM salesman, R Blair Smith, on a flight and the two began talking about computerised booking. In 1953 each booking still meant phone calls and clerks updating boards and cards by hand. In 1959 American and IBM signed up to build the system, borrowing ideas from IBM's work on the SAGE air-defence network. In 1960 the first computers were installed at Briarcliff Manor, New York, for testing. In 1964 SABRE took over all of American's reservations, linking agents across the country, the largest commercial real-time computer system in the world. In 1976 American began placing SABRE terminals in travel agents' offices.`,facts:[`SABRE and its nationwide network cost about $40 million (1964).`,`The 1964 system ran on two IBM 7090 computers, one a standby for the other.`]},"tech:timesale":{intro:`Time-sharing lets one big computer serve many people at once from typewriter terminals, switching between them so quickly that each seems to have the machine alone. Selling that time by the hour over a telephone line means a small engineering firm, a bank branch or a school can use a computer without buying one.`,how:`A customer rents a teleprinter and a modem and dials the service on an ordinary phone line, logs in with an account number and types programs in a simple language such as BASIC. The computer gives each user a short slice of time in turn and keeps their files on disc between sessions. The monthly bill counts the hours connected, the seconds of processor time used and the files stored.`,history:`In 1961 Fernando Corbató's team at MIT demonstrated the Compatible Time-Sharing System, several users sharing one IBM computer. In 1964 John Kemeny and Thomas Kurtz at Dartmouth College opened their time-sharing system on a General Electric computer, with BASIC as its language, so that every student could program. In 1965 General Electric began selling time on a copy of the Dartmouth system to businesses. In 1966 Tymshare was founded in California to sell time-sharing. In 1969 an Ohio life-insurance company set up a computer arm to sell time on its machines, under the name CompuServe. In 1971 Tymshare opened Tymnet, its own network of local dial-in points.`,facts:[`Dartmouth's first BASIC programs ran at about 4 am on 1 May 1964.`,`A standard Bell 103 modem carried 300 bits a second (1962).`]},"tech:swproducts":{intro:`Packaged software is a program written once and sold or leased to many customers, instead of being written by each company for itself or given away with the hardware. While the computer makers include their programs free, independent firms can hardly compete; once programs carry their own price, selling software becomes a business of its own.`,how:`A software house writes a general program for a common job, such as managing files, writing reports or sorting data, and leases it for a monthly fee with manuals, training and updates. Because the same code serves hundreds of customers, each pays far less than it would cost to write from scratch, and every extra copy costs the seller almost nothing to make.`,history:`In 1965 Applied Data Research of New Jersey began selling Autoflow, a program that drew flowcharts of existing programs. In 1967 Informatics launched Mark IV, a file-management package that became one of the best sellers of its era. In 1968 ADR's Martin Goetz received a US patent for a sorting program, often called the first software patent. In 1969 IBM, facing a US government antitrust suit, announced that it would charge separately for most of its software and services. In 1976 Bill Gates wrote an open letter to computer hobbyists, complaining that most users of his firm's BASIC had copied it rather than paid.`,facts:[`Gates reckoned in 1976 that royalties made his time on Altair BASIC worth under $2 an hour.`,`The US government's antitrust suit against IBM was filed in January 1969.`]},"tech:mrp":{intro:`Material requirements planning works out, from a plan of what a factory will build and when, exactly which parts and materials it must order or make, how many, and by what date. It replaces rules of thumb and big safety stocks with arithmetic, and needs a computer because a single product can contain thousands of parts.`,how:`The program starts from the master schedule, say 200 tractors in week 12, and explodes each through its bill of materials: the list of assemblies, sub-assemblies and parts it is made of. It subtracts stock on hand and orders already placed, then sets each need back by its lead time, so a gearbox needed in week 12 that takes three weeks to make is started in week 9. The output is a dated list of orders to place.`,history:`In 1964 Joseph Orlicky, an engineer at the farm-machinery maker J I Case in Racine, Wisconsin, ran one of the first computerised MRP systems on IBM equipment. In 1972 IBM published its COPICS plan for factory software, which spread MRP among its manufacturing customers. In 1975 Orlicky's book Material Requirements Planning became the standard guide. By 1975 about 700 US companies were using MRP, by Orlicky's count.`,facts:[`Orlicky counted roughly 700 US firms using MRP in 1975.`,`A farm tractor of 1964 could need thousands of separate parts, each with its own lead time.`]},"tech:rtfinance":{intro:`Real-time financial accounting posts each business event the moment it is typed in, so the books, the stock figures and the customer's bill stay in step all day rather than waiting for the night's batch run. Clerks work at screens instead of filling in forms for the punch room, and managers can see each day's figures as they happen.`,how:`Every transaction, a sale, a delivery, an invoice, is entered once at a terminal and written straight into shared files. The program updates every ledger it touches: the customer's account, the general ledger and the stock record. Standard modules for accounts, purchasing and stock replace the custom programs each company used to write, and each customer sets them up with tables of options rather than new code.`,history:`In 1972 five former IBM engineers, Dietmar Hopp, Hasso Plattner, Claus Wellenreuther, Klaus Tschira and Hans-Werner Hector, founded SAP in Weinheim, Germany. In 1972 their first customer was the German arm of ICI, the British chemicals company, and they wrote their programs at night on the clients' own computers. In 1973 they finished RF, a real-time financial accounting system that worked through screens rather than punched cards. In 1979 SAP brought out R/2, a mainframe suite that tied accounting to purchasing, stock and production. By 1980 about half of Germany's hundred largest industrial firms were SAP customers.`,facts:[`SAP began in 1972 with no computer of its own.`,`R/2 (1979) ran on IBM and Siemens mainframes.`]},"tech:edi":{intro:`Electronic data interchange lets one company's computer send business documents, orders, invoices and shipping notices, straight to another company's computer in a fixed, agreed layout. Nobody retypes the figures, so documents arrive in minutes instead of days, with far fewer mistakes.`,how:`Both sides follow a standard: each document is broken into segments and fields in a set order, such as buyer, item number, quantity and price. The buyer's system writes the order in that format and sends it over a telephone line, often through a value-added network that acts as a post office, holding messages in electronic mailboxes until each firm collects them. The seller's system reads the order straight into its own files.`,history:`The idea grew out of the 1948 Berlin Airlift, where US Army officer Edward Guilbert devised standard cargo manifests that could be sent by telex and radio to keep track of the flood of supplies. In 1968 US railway, road haulage, shipping and airline firms formed the Transportation Data Coordinating Committee to agree common electronic formats. In 1975 the committee published its first EDI standards for transport documents. In 1979 the American National Standards Institute chartered its X12 committee to write EDI standards for every industry. In 1987 the United Nations adopted EDIFACT as an international standard for trade documents.`,facts:[`ANSI's X12 committee for EDI standards was chartered in 1979.`,`The UN's EDIFACT standard for international trade documents was adopted in 1987.`]},"tech:mrp2":{intro:`Manufacturing resource planning, MRP II, widens material planning to the whole factory. It checks whether there are enough machines, workers and money to carry out the plan, and links production with purchasing, sales forecasts and the accounts, so the whole business works from one set of numbers.`,how:`On top of the parts explosion, MRP II adds capacity planning, which loads each order onto the work centres it will pass through and shows where a machine or shift is overbooked. Shop-floor control tracks jobs as they move, and the plan is costed so finance can see the cash it needs. Managers can run what-if simulations: what happens if the big order comes a month early?`,history:`In 1967 George Plossl and Oliver Wight wrote Production and Inventory Control, a standard textbook of the trade. In 1972 Sandra Kurtzig founded ASK Computer Systems in California, whose MANMAN software brought manufacturing planning to Hewlett-Packard minicomputers. In 1981 Wight's book MRP II: Unlocking America's Productivity Potential spread the name and the idea, and his firm taught it in seminars across the US. In 1981 ASK went public, and Kurtzig became one of the first women to take a technology company to the stock market.`,facts:[`Sandra Kurtzig started ASK with about $2,000 in 1972.`,`Wight's MRP II book appeared in 1981, aimed at factory managers rather than programmers.`]},"tech:hrms":{intro:`A human resources management system keeps everything a company knows about its staff in one program: hiring, job history, pay, holidays, benefits and pensions. Instead of the payroll department owning the data on a mainframe, personnel managers can look things up and change them themselves.`,how:`At its heart is one employee database. Around it sit modules for recruitment, salaries and grades, benefits, time off and payroll, all sharing the same records, so a pay rise entered once flows into the next pay slip and the pension sums. Running on networked PCs and servers with graphical screens, it lets HR staff build their own reports instead of waiting for the IT department.`,history:`In 1972 Dave Duffield co-founded Integral Systems, which sold HR and payroll software for mainframes. In 1987 Duffield and Ken Morris founded PeopleSoft in California to build the same kind of software for networks of smaller computers. In 1989 PeopleSoft shipped its first product, PeopleSoft HRMS, among the first HR packages designed for client-server networks. In 2005 Oracle completed a hostile takeover of PeopleSoft after a fight of 18 months. In 2005 Duffield and Aneel Bhusri founded Workday to rebuild HR software as a web service.`,facts:[`Oracle paid about $10.3 billion for PeopleSoft (2005).`,`Oracle's takeover battle for PeopleSoft lasted about 18 months, from 2003 to 2005.`]},"tech:erp":{intro:`Enterprise resource planning software runs a whole company's main processes, finance, purchasing, sales, stock, manufacturing and HR, as one integrated system with one database. Client-server ERP moves this off the mainframe: the database sits on a server, and staff work in graphical programs on their PCs.`,how:`Each department works in its own module, but every module reads and writes the same tables, so a sales order instantly reserves stock, schedules production and books the revenue. The package is generic: a firm sets thousands of options to match its own way of working, or changes its way of working to fit the software. Installing it takes months or years and armies of consultants, and often costs far more than the licences.`,history:`In 1990 analysts at the Gartner Group coined the name enterprise resource planning for systems that went beyond MRP II. In 1992 SAP released R/3, a client-server version of its suite that ran on Unix servers with PCs as screens. In 1996 FoxMeyer, a large US drug distributor, went bankrupt after a troubled ERP project, which it blamed in part for its failure. In 1999 fear of the year 2000 bug drove many companies to replace old systems with ERP. In 1999 Hershey's rushed ERP launch left it unable to deliver sweets to shops in time for Halloween.`,facts:[`Hershey failed to ship about $100 million of orders after its ERP launch (1999).`,`SAP's R/3 suite was released in 1992.`]},"tech:bizintel":{intro:`Business intelligence copies a company's records from all its separate systems into one big store built for answering questions rather than for daily work. Managers can then slice the figures any way they like, sales by region, by month, by product, and spot trends without asking a programmer to write a report.`,how:`Each night extract programs pull data out of the order, billing and stock systems, clean it and load it into a data warehouse. Figures are arranged as a cube with dimensions such as time, place and product, so a user can drill down from a yearly total to one shop on one Tuesday. Point-and-click tools on the PC turn questions into database queries and draw the answers as tables and charts.`,history:`In 1958 IBM researcher Hans Peter Luhn wrote a paper titled "A Business Intelligence System", about machines that would sort and send documents to the people who needed them. In 1988 Barry Devlin and Paul Murphy of IBM described a "business data warehouse" fed from a company's working systems. In 1990 Bernard Liautaud and Denis Payre founded Business Objects in France to sell query tools to ordinary managers. In 1992 Bill Inmon's book Building the Data Warehouse set out how to build one. In 1993 Edgar Codd, inventor of the relational database, published rules for online analytical processing, OLAP.`,facts:[`Codd's 1993 OLAP paper set out 12 rules for analysis tools.`,`Luhn's 1958 paper appeared in the IBM Journal of Research and Development.`]},"tech:smbbooks":{intro:`Small-business accounting software brings bookkeeping to people who are not accountants: shopkeepers, plumbers, small offices. Instead of debits, credits and ledgers, the screen shows familiar things, a cheque book, an invoice, a list of bills to pay, and the program quietly does the double-entry bookkeeping behind them.`,how:`The owner writes a cheque or an invoice on a screen that looks like the paper form; the program files each entry in the right accounts, tracks who owes what, and prints profit-and-loss statements and tax summaries. It runs on an ordinary PC and costs about as much as a few hours of an accountant's time, and the same company sells printed cheques and forms that fit the printer.`,history:`In 1978 Peachtree Software in Atlanta began selling accounting programs for early microcomputers. In 1981 David Goldman and partners founded Sage in Newcastle upon Tyne, England, to write accounts software for small firms. In 1983 Scott Cook and Tom Proulx founded Intuit in California, and in 1984 it shipped Quicken, a home-finance program that copied the look of a cheque book. In 1992 Intuit launched QuickBooks, having found that many Quicken buyers were using it to run a business. In 1994 Microsoft agreed to buy Intuit for about $1.5 billion in shares. In 1995 Microsoft dropped the deal after the US Justice Department sued to block it.`,facts:[`Sage was founded in Newcastle upon Tyne in 1981.`,`Intuit went public in 1993, a year after launching QuickBooks.`]},"tech:crmapps":{intro:`Sales force automation software gives a sales team one shared database of customers, contacts, calls, quotes and deals in progress. Each salesperson keeps notes on a laptop that copies its changes to the central server, and managers can see the whole pipeline of likely sales and forecast the quarter.`,how:`Every account has a record of its people, past orders and each call and meeting. Deals move through stages, from lead to quote to signed contract, each with a value and a chance of closing; adding them up gives the forecast. Because salespeople travel, the laptop copy works offline and synchronises when it dials in. The same idea grows to cover marketing and customer service: customer relationship management, or CRM.`,history:`In 1987 Pat Sullivan and Mike Muhney released ACT!, a contact manager for salespeople on PCs. In 1993 Tom Siebel, a former star salesman at Oracle, founded Siebel Systems in California. In 1995 Siebel shipped its first sales package, built for big companies with large sales forces. In 2000 Siebel's revenue reached about $1.8 billion, making it one of the fastest-growing software firms ever. In 2006 Oracle completed its purchase of Siebel.`,facts:[`Siebel's revenue was about $1.8 billion in 2000.`,`Oracle paid about $5.85 billion for Siebel (2006).`]},"tech:ondemand":{intro:`Software as a service runs a business program on the vendor's own servers, and customers use it through a web browser for a monthly fee per user. There is nothing to install and no server to buy, and every customer has the newest version at once, because there is only one copy.`,how:`One large system serves many customer companies at once, keeping each one's data walled off inside shared databases, a multi-tenant design. The vendor upgrades everyone together several times a year. Customers can start with a few users and a credit card, without a big licence fee or a long installation project, and the vendor earns only as long as they keep paying, so it must keep them happy.`,history:`In 1998 Evan Goldberg founded NetLedger, offering accounting on the web, with money from Oracle's Larry Ellison. In 1999 Marc Benioff, a former Oracle executive, founded Salesforce in a flat in San Francisco with three colleagues. In 2000 Salesforce launched its service with a "No Software" campaign, even hiring actors to stage a mock protest outside a Siebel conference. In 2004 Salesforce went public on the New York Stock Exchange. By 2009 Salesforce's yearly revenue had passed $1 billion.`,facts:[`Salesforce's stock-market ticker symbol was CRM (2004).`,`By 2009 Salesforce earned over $1 billion a year.`]},"tech:appmarket":{intro:`A business app marketplace is an online shop inside a software service, where other companies sell add-ons that run on the same platform and use the same data. Customers get tools the vendor never built, partners get customers, and every add-on a company installs makes the service harder to leave.`,how:`The vendor publishes programming interfaces and a platform so outside developers can build apps that live inside the service, store data in its databases and appear in its menus. The marketplace lists them with ratings and reviews and installs them in a few clicks. The vendor checks each app for security and often takes a share of the sales.`,history:`In 2005 Salesforce launched AppExchange, an online directory of add-ons for its service. In 2007 it introduced Force.com, which let developers build whole applications on Salesforce's servers using Apex, its own programming language. In 2008 Google opened App Engine, letting developers run web apps on Google's computers. In 2010 Google opened the Google Apps Marketplace, selling add-ons for its business mail and documents service.`,facts:[`Salesforce's Apex language, introduced in 2007, ran only on Salesforce's servers.`,`Google Apps Marketplace opened in March 2010.`]},"tech:wpmachine":{intro:`A word processing machine is an electric typewriter with a memory. The typist's keystrokes are recorded as well as printed, so a mistake can be fixed by retyping only the wrong line, and a finished letter can be played back to type perfect copies at full speed with nobody at the keyboard.`,how:`IBM's Magnetic Tape Selectric Typewriter sits beside a cabinet of magnetic tape. Each keystroke is stored on the tape as it is typed onto paper. To correct a mistake the typist winds the tape back to it and types over it; to make the final copy, the machine reads the tape and drives the golf-ball typing head by itself. Standard paragraphs can be stored once and combined into many letters.`,history:`In 1961 IBM launched the Selectric typewriter, which typed with a spinning ball of letters instead of a basket of type bars. In 1964 IBM added magnetic tape to it as the MT/ST, aimed at offices that sent out many similar letters. In 1969 Evelyn Berezin founded Redactron in New York to build a word processor with its own electronics. In 1969 IBM replaced the tape with magnetic cards in the MC/ST, each card holding about one typed page. In 1971 Redactron shipped its Data Secretary, built from integrated circuits.`,facts:[`An MC/ST magnetic card held about one typed page (1969).`,`The Selectric's golf-ball head could be swapped in seconds to change typeface (1961).`]},"tech:screenwp":{intro:`A screen word processor shows the text on a display before anything is printed. The writer can scroll through a whole document, delete, insert and move paragraphs, and search for words, then print a clean copy only when it is right. It turns the secretary's typewriter into a small computer that does nothing but text.`,how:`A desk terminal with a keyboard and screen shows part of the page, with a cursor marking where typing goes. The text is stored on floppy discs or a shared hard disc. Menus on the screen offer commands such as insert, delete, move and copy, so editing never means retyping, and a daisy-wheel printer turns out letter-quality pages.`,history:`In 1976 Wang Laboratories of Lowell, Massachusetts, showed its WPS word processor with a screen at an office equipment show in New York, where crowds queued to see it. In 1977 Wang's OIS let many screens share one central computer and disc store. In 1978 MicroPro released WordStar, written by Rob Barnaby, which brought screen word processing to cheap CP/M microcomputers. In 1982 Satellite Software International released WordPerfect for the IBM PC.`,facts:[`Rob Barnaby wrote WordStar in assembly language in about four months (1978).`,`The Osborne 1 portable computer came with WordStar included (1981).`]},"tech:spreadsheet":{intro:`An electronic spreadsheet turns the computer screen into a grid of rows and columns, like an accountant's ruled paper. Each cell holds a number, a label or a formula that uses other cells, and when any number changes, every formula that depends on it recalculates at once. Asking what happens if sales rise ten per cent takes seconds instead of an afternoon.`,how:`Cells are named by column letter and row number, such as B4. A formula, for example the sum of B1 to B3, is stored in a cell and shown as its result. When an entry changes the program recalculates the sheet, so a budget, a loan table or a sales forecast updates instantly. The screen is a window that scrolls over a sheet much larger than itself.`,history:`In 1978 Dan Bricklin, a student at Harvard Business School, imagined a calculator whose display was a whole sheet of figures, and built a rough version in BASIC. In 1979 he and programmer Bob Frankston, working as Software Arts, finished VisiCalc for the Apple II, sold by Dan Fylstra's Personal Software. In 1979 software was hard to patent in the US, and they never patented the idea. In 1981 VisiCalc came to the new IBM PC. In 1985 Lotus bought Software Arts, and VisiCalc was soon withdrawn.`,facts:[`VisiCalc on the Apple II needed a machine with 32 KB of memory (1979).`,`VisiCalc's name, chosen in 1979, stood for visible calculator.`]},"tech:integrated":{intro:`An integrated spreadsheet combines a spreadsheet, business charts and a simple database in one program, sharing the same data and commands. Written to use all the memory and speed of the new 16-bit office PCs, it makes the grid feel instant and turns a column of figures into a bar chart with a few keystrokes.`,how:`Lotus 1-2-3 is written in assembly language and draws its screen straight into the PC's video memory, which makes it far faster than programs that go through the operating system. A slash key opens a menu across the top of the screen. Macros let users record keystrokes and replay them, so a sheet can become a small application. Charts and database sorting work on the same cells.`,history:`In 1982 Mitch Kapor, who had written charting programs for VisiCalc's publisher, founded Lotus Development in Cambridge, Massachusetts, with programmer Jonathan Sachs. In 1983 Lotus released 1-2-3 for the IBM PC, and it overtook VisiCalc within months. In 1983 Lotus took about $53 million in sales, an unheard-of first year for a software company. By 1984 many companies were buying IBM PCs and compatibles just to run 1-2-3. In 1985 Microsoft brought out Excel for the Apple Macintosh.`,facts:[`Lotus 1-2-3 sold for $495 (1983).`,`Lotus's sales roughly tripled to about $156 million in 1984.`]},"tech:wysiwyg":{intro:`WYSIWYG, short for "what you see is what you get", means the screen shows a document as it will look when printed: real typefaces in different sizes, bold and italics, proper margins and line breaks. It needs a screen made of dots that can draw any shape, rather than one that shows only fixed rows of letters.`,how:`The Xerox Alto has a tall bit-mapped display: every dot on the screen is a bit in memory, so software can draw letters in any font. Bravo keeps the text as a chain of pieces, each with its formatting attached, and redraws only the lines that change, quickly enough to keep up with typing. A mouse selects the text, and a laser printer on the office network prints what the screen shows.`,history:`In 1973 Xerox's Palo Alto Research Center built the Alto, a personal computer with a page-shaped bit-mapped screen and a mouse. In 1974 Butler Lampson and Charles Simonyi wrote Bravo, the first WYSIWYG document editor, for the Alto. In 1975 Larry Tesler and Tim Mott built Gypsy, a simpler version for the publisher Ginn, with cut and paste done by mouse. In 1981 Xerox sold the idea as part of its Star office workstation. In 1984 Apple's Macintosh brought WYSIWYG writing to a much cheaper computer with MacWrite.`,facts:[`The Alto's screen was 606 by 808 dots, portrait like a page (1973).`,`A Xerox Star workstation cost about $16,000 (1981).`,`The first Macintosh cost $2,495 (1984).`]},"tech:dtp":{intro:`Desktop publishing lets one person with a personal computer and a laser printer design and print pages that once needed typesetters, paste-up artists and a print shop: newsletters, brochures, magazines and books. Text and pictures are arranged on screen exactly as they will print, in proper typefaces.`,how:`Three things fit together. A page layout program arranges columns of text, headlines and pictures on screen. A page description language, PostScript, describes each page as mathematical outlines of letters and shapes that any printer can draw at its own sharpness. A laser printer with its own processor turns that description into 300 dots per inch on paper, and the same file can go to a high-resolution typesetter for the final print run.`,history:`In 1982 John Warnock and Chuck Geschke left Xerox PARC and founded Adobe Systems in California. In 1984 Adobe released PostScript, its language for describing printed pages. In 1985 Apple launched the LaserWriter, the first printer with PostScript built in, and Aldus released PageMaker for the Macintosh. In 1985 Aldus's founder, Paul Brainerd, coined the phrase desktop publishing. In 1987 Quark released QuarkXPress, aimed at professional magazine and newspaper designers.`,facts:[`The LaserWriter printed 300 dots per inch at about 8 pages a minute (1985).`,`The LaserWriter had 1.5 MB of memory, three times a 512K Macintosh (1985).`]},"tech:slides":{intro:`Presentation software lets a speaker build slides on a personal computer: titles, bullet points, charts and pictures in a consistent design. The results can be printed onto transparent sheets for an overhead projector or sent away to be made into 35 mm photographic slides, without waiting days for a graphics department.`,how:`Each slide is built on a template, so fonts, colours and backgrounds stay consistent. In an outline view the speaker writes the talk as headings and points, which flow onto the slides; a slide sorter shows them small so they can be dragged into a new order. Speaker's notes and handouts for the audience print from the same file.`,history:`In 1984 Robert Gaskins joined Forethought, a small Californian firm, and with programmer Dennis Austin designed a slide program first called Presenter. In 1986 Software Publishing's Harvard Graphics gave IBM PC users business charts and slides. In 1987 Forethought released PowerPoint 1.0 for the Macintosh, in black and white, and Microsoft bought the company three months later. In 1988 PowerPoint 2.0 added colour and could send files to be made into 35 mm slides. In 1990 PowerPoint came to Windows 3.0.`,facts:[`PowerPoint 1.0 for the Macintosh printed in black and white only (1987).`,`Forethought became Microsoft's first big software acquisition (1987).`]},"tech:officesuite":{intro:`An office suite bundles the main desk programs, a word processor, a spreadsheet and a presentation program, in one box for not much more than the price of one. The programs share menus, commands and a common look, and can paste a chart or a table from one into another.`,how:`Each program is still separate, but they are designed to work alike, so learning one helps with the others, and they exchange data: a spreadsheet table can sit inside a letter and update when the figures change. For the seller, the bundle beats rivals who make only one of the parts; for companies it means one purchase, one licence deal and one set of training courses.`,history:`In 1989 Microsoft released Office for the Macintosh, putting Word, Excel and PowerPoint in one package. In 1990 Office came to Windows, just as Windows 3.0 made graphical PCs popular. In 1994 Novell bought WordPerfect and the Quattro Pro spreadsheet to build a rival suite. In 1996 Novell sold WordPerfect to Corel for far less than it had paid. In 2000 Sun Microsystems released the code of StarOffice, a German suite it had bought, as the free OpenOffice.`,facts:[`Novell's purchase of WordPerfect closed at about $855 million in shares (1994).`,`Sun bought Star Division, maker of StarOffice, in 1999.`]},"tech:email":{intro:`Network email lets a person on one computer send a message to someone on another computer across a network, where it waits in their mailbox until they read it. The address names both the person and the machine, joined by an @ sign, and a message crosses the country in seconds for almost nothing.`,how:`Each computer runs a mail program that writes messages into users' mailbox files. To deliver to another machine, it hands the message to a file-transfer program that sends it across the network and adds it to the other computer's mailbox. Messages are plain text with a few header lines, such as From, To, Subject and Date, that programs can read to sort, reply and forward.`,history:`In 1965 MIT's time-sharing system gained a MAIL command, letting users of the same computer leave each other messages. In 1971 Ray Tomlinson at BBN in Cambridge, Massachusetts, sent the first messages between two computers on the ARPANET, choosing the @ sign to separate the user from the machine. In 1975 John Vittal's MSG program added reply and forward commands. In 1976 Queen Elizabeth II sent an email from a defence research centre at Malvern. In 1982 Jon Postel published SMTP, the Simple Mail Transfer Protocol, which set the rules for mail servers on the Internet.`,facts:[`Tomlinson said his first test message was forgettable, something like QWERTYUIOP (1971).`,`The Queen's 1976 email account was named HME2.`,`SMTP was defined in RFC 821 in 1982.`]},"tech:groupware":{intro:`Groupware gives a whole organisation shared electronic workspaces: email, calendars and databases of documents that many people can read, discuss and update. Discussions, project files and forms that used to travel round on paper live on the network, where anyone with access can find them.`,how:`Lotus Notes keeps documents in databases on servers and copies changes between servers and laptops, called replication, so people can work offline and catch up later. A database can hold anything: a sales log, a discussion forum, a staff directory. Simple forms and formulas let departments build their own small applications without programmers, and security settings control who can see and change what.`,history:`In 1973 the PLATO system at the University of Illinois gained Notes, an online discussion board for its users. In 1984 Ray Ozzie, who had used PLATO as a student, founded Iris Associates to build a networked version for business, paid for by Lotus. In 1989 Lotus shipped Notes 1.0. In 1989, before it shipped, the accounting firm Price Waterhouse ordered 10,000 copies. In 1995 IBM bought Lotus, chiefly to get Notes. In 1996 Microsoft launched Exchange Server to compete.`,facts:[`Price Waterhouse ordered 10,000 copies of Notes before release (1989).`,`IBM paid about $3.5 billion for Lotus (1995).`]},"tech:webmail":{intro:`Webmail puts a person's mailbox on a website. Anyone can sign up free and then read and send mail from any computer with a web browser, at home, at work or in a library, without setting up a mail program. The service pays for itself with advertisements shown beside the messages.`,how:`Mail is stored on the provider's servers, and web pages built on the fly show the inbox, open messages and offer a form for writing new ones. Every message sent from a free account carries a short advert for the service at the bottom, so each email recruits new users. Accounts come with a few megabytes of storage, so users must delete old mail to make room.`,history:`In 1996 Sabeer Bhatia and Jack Smith launched Hotmail on 4 July, American Independence Day, its name a nod to HTML. In 1997 Microsoft bought Hotmail, which by then had about 9 million users. In 1997 Yahoo opened Yahoo Mail, built on RocketMail from the firm Four11, which it had bought. In 2004 Google announced Gmail on 1 April with a gigabyte of storage each, when rivals offered a few megabytes, and many people took it for an April Fool's joke.`,facts:[`Hotmail signed up about 1 million users in its first six months (1996).`,`Gmail offered 1,000 MB when Hotmail gave 2 MB (2004).`]},"tech:clouddocs":{intro:`An online office runs word processing and spreadsheets inside the web browser, with the documents kept on the provider's servers. Nothing needs installing, a file opens from any computer, and several people can type in the same document at once, each seeing the others' changes appear within seconds.`,how:`The editor is a web page written in JavaScript that sends each change to the server in the background. The server merges the edits of everyone working on the file, settles clashes and sends the result back out, while keeping a revision history so old versions can be restored. Sharing is by email address or link rather than by mailing attachments around.`,history:`In 2005 the small firm Upstartle launched Writely, an online word processor. In 2006 Google bought Writely and combined it with its own online spreadsheet as Google Docs & Spreadsheets. In 2007 Google began selling its office tools to companies as Google Apps Premier Edition, and later that year added presentations. In 2010 Microsoft released free web versions of Word, Excel and PowerPoint alongside Office 2010. In 2011 Microsoft launched Office 365, renting its suite and online services by subscription.`,facts:[`Google Apps Premier Edition cost $50 per user per year (2007).`,`Google Docs & Spreadsheets was free for personal use when it launched in 2006.`]},"tech:onlineservice":{intro:`A dial-up online service lets home computer owners connect to a big central computer through a modem and an ordinary telephone line. Once connected they can read news, weather and share prices, send electronic mail, chat with other members and play games, all as screens of text, and they pay for every hour they stay on.`,how:`The service runs on time-sharing mainframes, often the same ones businesses rent during the day, sold cheaply to home users in the evenings when they would sit idle. Members dial a local access number on a data network, avoiding long-distance charges, then type commands or pick from numbered menus. Text arrives at 300 bits per second, about 30 letters a second, slower than most people read.`,history:`In 1979 The Source opened in the US, selling news, mail and chat to home computer owners. In 1979 CompuServe began MicroNET, selling evening time on its business computers to home users through Radio Shack shops. In 1980 CompuServe started its CB Simulator, live chat rooms named after citizens' band radio, and the tax firm H&R Block bought the company. In 1987 CompuServe created the GIF format so members could swap pictures quickly. In 1989 CompuServe linked its mail to the Internet, so members could write to university and research addresses.`,facts:[`A 300-bit-per-second modem delivered about 30 characters a second (1979).`,`CompuServe published the GIF picture format in 1987.`]},"tech:guionline":{intro:`A point-and-click online service replaces typed commands with pictures, icons and menus drawn by software on the member's own computer. Only the content travels down the telephone line, so even a slow modem feels quick, and the service is friendly enough for families who have never used a computer network.`,how:`Members install a program, often from a free disc, that draws the screens, buttons and windows on their own machine. The central computers send only text and compact drawing instructions, which the program turns into pictures. Members chat, send mail, play games with each other and browse news and shops, and the same program handles dialling and logging in, so there is nothing to set up.`,history:`In 1985 Quantum Computer Services, with Jim Kimsey, Steve Case and Marc Seriff among its leaders, launched Quantum Link for owners of the Commodore 64. In 1988 IBM and the retailer Sears began Prodigy, which drew pictures on screen and carried advertising along the bottom. In 1989 Quantum launched America Online for Apple computers, and in 1991 the company renamed itself after it. In 1993 AOL began mailing free trial discs by the million and opened a gateway to Usenet discussion groups. In 1994 AOL passed one million members.`,facts:[`AOL reached one million members in 1994.`,`Prodigy was owned jointly by IBM and Sears (1988).`]},"tech:flatrate":{intro:`A flat-rate plan charges one fixed price a month for unlimited time online, instead of a meter that runs for every hour connected. Customers who used to log off quickly to save money start staying on for whole evenings, and leave connections open while they eat or even sleep.`,how:`The provider no longer earns more from heavy users, so its income depends on signing up members and keeping the cost of serving each one low. Every member online ties up a modem and a telephone line at the provider's end, so when usage multiplies, members hear busy signals unless the company buys equipment fast. Income shifts towards advertising and deals with shops on the service.`,history:`In 1990 Prodigy charged $9.95 a month for its main services, and in 1991 it added fees for members who sent more than a set number of emails. In 1996 AT&T launched its WorldNet internet service with unlimited use for $19.95 a month. In 1996 AOL switched to unlimited use for $19.95 a month in December, and usage rose so sharply that members met weeks of busy signals. In 1997 AOL agreed with dozens of US states to offer refunds to members who could not get through.`,facts:[`AT&T WorldNet sold unlimited internet access for $19.95 a month (1996).`,`AOL moved to flat-rate pricing in December 1996.`]},"tech:webdirectory":{intro:`A web directory is a catalogue of websites sorted by people into topics and sub-topics, like a library's subject shelves: arts, business, computers, science. A portal wraps the directory in news, weather, share prices, email and chat, so people make it their browser's home page and start every visit to the web there.`,how:`Editors visit sites submitted by their owners, judge them and file them in the hierarchy with a short description. Users either click down through the categories or search the descriptions. Because millions of people pass through the front page every day, the portal earns its money by selling advertising there, and adds more services to keep visitors from leaving.`,history:`In 1994 Stanford University students Jerry Yang and David Filo started a list of their favourite websites, Jerry and David's Guide to the World Wide Web, and renamed it Yahoo that spring. In 1995 they incorporated Yahoo with money from the venture firm Sequoia Capital. In 1996 Yahoo went public. In 1998 the Open Directory Project began building a rival directory with unpaid volunteer editors. In 2000 Yahoo's shares peaked, giving it a market value of about $125 billion.`,facts:[`Yahoo's market value peaked near $125 billion in January 2000.`,`Yahoo began as a list of links on Stanford's computers in 1994.`]},"tech:banners":{intro:`A banner ad is a small rectangular picture across the top or side of a web page that takes the reader to the advertiser's own site when clicked. Websites sell them like magazine pages, by the thousand times they are shown, which gives the young web its first way to make money from free content.`,how:`The site agrees a price per thousand views, called CPM, and shows the advertiser's image the agreed number of times. Because every click is recorded, advertisers can see exactly how many readers responded, the click-through rate, something print and television cannot measure. Animated GIF pictures make the banners move to catch the eye.`,history:`In 1994 HotWired, the online offshoot of Wired magazine, sold the first banner ads, among them an AT&T banner that asked "Have you ever clicked your mouse right HERE?" In 1996 the newly formed Internet Advertising Bureau set standard banner sizes so one advert could run on many sites. In 1997 US online advertising came close to $1 billion. In 2000 US internet advertising reached about $8 billion, but typical click-through rates had fallen well below one per cent.`,facts:[`About 44 per cent of people who saw AT&T's first banner clicked it (1994).`,`The standard full banner set in 1996 measured 468 by 60 pixels.`,`US online advertising reached about $8 billion in 2000.`]},"tech:adserving":{intro:`An ad server is a central system that picks which advertisement to show on each web page as it loads, for thousands of websites at once. Because it sees the same browser on many sites, it can count how often each person has seen an advert, limit repeats and choose adverts that suit what that person seems interested in.`,how:`Each member site puts a small tag in its pages that asks the network's servers for an advert. The network reads a cookie, a small identifying file it left in the browser earlier, checks that browser's history against the advertisers' targets, picks an advert and logs the view. Advertisers buy one campaign that runs across hundreds of sites, and the network shares the money with the publishers.`,history:`In 1994 Lou Montulli at Netscape invented the cookie, a way for a website to recognise a returning browser. In 1996 Kevin O'Connor and Dwight Merriman founded DoubleClick in New York to sell and serve adverts for a network of sites. In 1999 DoubleClick bought Abacus Direct, a database of the names and addresses of catalogue shoppers. In 2000 a public outcry and a US Federal Trade Commission inquiry made DoubleClick drop its plan to link those names to web browsing. In 2008 Google completed its purchase of DoubleClick after a long review by regulators.`,facts:[`Netscape Navigator first supported cookies in 1994.`,`Google paid $3.1 billion for DoubleClick, completing the deal in 2008.`]},"tech:crawler":{intro:`Full-text web search uses a program called a crawler, or spider, that downloads web pages, follows every link to more pages and records every word on each one in a giant index. A user types a few words and the engine lists the pages that contain them, out of millions, in a second or two.`,how:`The crawler keeps a list of addresses to visit, fetches each page, stores its text and adds its links to the list. An indexer builds an inverted index: for every word, a list of the pages that contain it, like the index at the back of a book. A query looks up each word's list, keeps the pages found in all of them and ranks them by how often and where the words appear.`,history:`In 1990 Alan Emtage at McGill University in Montreal built Archie, which searched the lists of files kept on FTP sites. In 1994 Brian Pinkerton at the University of Washington launched WebCrawler, the first engine to index the full text of every page it visited. In 1994 Michael Mauldin at Carnegie Mellon University started Lycos. In 1995 Digital Equipment Corporation launched AltaVista, which used DEC's fast Alpha computers to index far more pages than its rivals. In 1998 a study in the journal Science found that no engine covered more than about a third of the web.`,facts:[`AltaVista launched in December 1995 with an index of about 16 million pages.`,`No search engine indexed more than about a third of the web in 1998, one study found.`]},"tech:pagerank":{intro:`Link-analysis ranking judges a web page by the links pointing to it. A link counts as a vote, and a vote from a page that is itself much linked-to counts for more. Searches then put the most important pages first, which makes results far harder to fool by stuffing a page with repeated keywords.`,how:`Imagine a surfer who clicks links at random and now and then jumps to a random page. The share of time the surfer spends on each page is its PageRank. It is computed for the whole web at once by passing each page's score out along its links, over and over, until the numbers settle. The engine combines this with how well the page's text, and the words of links pointing to it, match the search.`,history:`In 1996 Stanford students Larry Page and Sergey Brin built BackRub, a search engine that ranked pages by the links pointing to them. In 1998 they founded Google, working from a garage in Menlo Park rented from Susan Wojcicki, after Andy Bechtolsheim, a founder of Sun Microsystems, wrote them a cheque for $100,000. In 2000 Google's index passed a billion pages. In 2001 Stanford University was granted the PageRank patent, which it licensed to Google. In 2005 Stanford sold the Google shares it had received for the licence for $336 million.`,facts:[`The 1998 Stanford prototype had indexed about 24 million pages.`,`Google's index passed 1 billion pages in 2000.`,`Stanford sold its 1.8 million Google shares for $336 million in 2005.`]},"tech:ppc":{intro:`Pay-per-click search advertising sells the top places in search results for particular words. An advertiser bids to appear whenever someone searches for, say, "cheap flights", and pays only when a searcher clicks through to its site. The adverts reach people at the very moment they are looking for something to buy.`,how:`Advertisers choose keywords and the most they will pay for each click. When someone searches, the highest bidders' listings appear first and each click costs the bid. Because advertisers pay only for real visits, even tiny firms can take part with a few dollars a day, and the search company earns money on every search that shows adverts.`,history:`In 1998 Bill Gross's start-up workshop Idealab launched GoTo.com, a search engine where websites bid for their place in the results. In 2000 GoTo began supplying its paid listings to big portals such as AOL, sharing the money with them. In 2000 Google introduced AdWords, selling small text adverts beside its results at a price per thousand views. In 2001 GoTo renamed itself Overture Services. In 2003 Yahoo bought Overture.`,facts:[`Google's AdWords launched in 2000 with about 350 advertisers.`,`Yahoo paid about $1.6 billion for Overture (2003).`]},"tech:adauction":{intro:`A quality-scored ad auction decides which search adverts appear, and in what order, by combining each advertiser's bid with how likely people are to click the advert. A cheaper advert that searchers find useful can beat a costly one they ignore, so the search engine earns more and users see adverts that actually help them.`,how:`For each advert the system estimates the chance it will be clicked, from its past click rate and how well it matches the search. Adverts are ranked by bid times that chance. Each winner pays only just enough to stay above the next advert, a generalised second-price auction, so there is little point bidding above what a click is really worth. The auction runs afresh for every search, in a fraction of a second.`,history:`In 2002 Google relaunched AdWords Select with pay-per-click bidding, ranking adverts by bid multiplied by click-through rate. In 2002 AOL chose Google rather than Overture to supply adverts to its search pages. In 2004 Google went public. In 2005 Google introduced a Quality Score that also judged the page an advert led to. In 2007 economists, among them Google's chief economist Hal Varian, published studies of how such auctions behave.`,facts:[`Google's revenue rose from about $440 million in 2002 to about $3.2 billion in 2004.`,`Google's shares were first sold at $85 each in 2004.`]},"tech:adsense":{intro:`A contextual ad network reads the text of a web page, works out what it is about and places adverts that match it: hiking boots beside an article on mountain walks. Any website, from a newspaper to a hobby blog, can sign up, show the adverts and receive a share of what the advertisers pay.`,how:`When a site joins, the network's software reads each page and picks out its main topics and keywords, much as a search engine does. Each time a reader loads the page, an auction among advertisers who bid on those keywords chooses the adverts; when a reader clicks, the advertiser pays and the site owner gets the larger share. Small sites that could never hire an advertising sales team can earn from their pages.`,history:`In 2003 Google bought Applied Semantics, a Los Angeles firm whose software grouped words by meaning. In 2003 Google launched AdSense, first for large content sites and then, from June, for any website that signed up. In 2004 adverts on partner sites brought in about half of Google's revenue. In 2005 Yahoo launched a rival network for websites, the Yahoo Publisher Network.`,facts:[`Google paid about $102 million for Applied Semantics (2003).`,`Google's partner sites earned it about $1.6 billion in 2004.`]},"tech:webcheckout":{intro:`Secure card checkout scrambles a customer's card number and address as they travel from the web browser to the shop's server, so anyone tapping the line sees only gibberish. A small padlock in the browser shows that the connection is encrypted and that the shop is who it claims to be.`,how:`The browser and server begin with a handshake. The server sends a certificate, signed by a trusted authority, that holds its public key. The browser uses that key to send a secret random number that only the server can unlock, and both sides turn it into a shared key for fast encryption of the rest of the visit. Addresses of secure pages begin with https.`,history:`In 1994 Netscape designed the Secure Sockets Layer, SSL, for its new browser, but kept its first version private because of flaws. In 1995 SSL 2.0 shipped in Netscape Navigator. In 1995 a French student, Damien Doligez, broke a 40-bit export-grade SSL key, the strongest the US then let browsers sold abroad use. In 1995 Berkeley students Ian Goldberg and David Wagner showed that Netscape's keys could be guessed because its random numbers were not random enough. In 1996 SSL 3.0 fixed the design's weaknesses. In 1999 the Internet Engineering Task Force made it a standard under a new name, TLS 1.0.`,facts:[`A 40-bit SSL key was cracked in about eight days by a student in 1995.`,`TLS 1.0 was published as an Internet standard in January 1999.`]},"tech:onlinestore":{intro:`An online store is a shop that exists only as a website: customers search a catalogue, read descriptions and reviews, put items in a basket and pay by card, and the goods arrive by post. With no shop floor it can list every book in print, far more than the biggest bookshop can hold.`,how:`The catalogue is a database of titles fed from publishers' and wholesalers' lists. Web pages built from it show each item with its price and how soon it ships, and a shopping basket follows the customer from page to page. When an order comes in, the store buys the book from a wholesaler, often within days, then packs and posts it. Customers' own reviews fill the pages for free.`,history:`In 1994 Jeff Bezos left the Wall Street firm D E Shaw and moved to Seattle to sell books on the web, choosing books because millions of titles were in print. In 1995 Amazon.com opened to the public and within a month had sold books to buyers in every US state and dozens of countries. In 1997 Amazon went public. In 1998 it began selling music CDs and videos. In 1999 Time magazine named Bezos its Person of the Year.`,facts:[`Amazon sold to all 50 US states and 45 countries in its first 30 days (1995).`,`Amazon's shares were first sold at $18 in 1997.`,`Amazon called itself Earth's Biggest Bookstore, listing over a million titles (1995).`]},"tech:auctions":{intro:`An online marketplace lets private people and small traders sell to one another through a website, often by auction: the seller lists an item with a description and a starting price, buyers bid until a deadline, and the highest bid wins. The site owns no stock and ships nothing; it charges a small fee for each listing and sale.`,how:`Sellers pay a listing fee and a percentage of the final price. Each auction page shows the current bid and the time left, and bidders can enter the most they will pay so the site raises their bid automatically, step by step. After the sale, buyer and seller arrange payment, usually by cheque or money order, and postage between themselves. Collectors of anything, from stamps to toys, find a national market.`,history:`In 1995 Pierre Omidyar, a software engineer, started AuctionWeb as part of his personal website in San Jose, California. In 1996 he began charging sellers a small fee, and the payments soon outgrew his day job's salary. In 1997 the site was renamed eBay, after Omidyar's consulting firm, Echo Bay Technology Group. In 1998 Meg Whitman became chief executive, and eBay went public in September. In 1998 its shares rose from $18 to about $47 on their first day.`,facts:[`eBay's shares rose from $18 to about $47 on their first trading day (1998).`,`The name eBay was chosen in 1997 because echobay.com was already taken.`]},"tech:reputation":{intro:`A feedback and ratings system lets buyers and sellers rate each other in public after every deal. Each member's record of good, neutral and bad ratings sits beside their name, so a stranger with hundreds of happy customers can be trusted with money sent by post before the goods arrive.`,how:`After a sale each side leaves a rating of plus one, zero or minus one and a short comment. The ratings add up to a score shown beside the member's name, often with a star whose colour changes at milestones. Because members want to keep a good record, most behave well; because a new account starts with nothing, cheats cannot simply start again and look trustworthy.`,history:`In 1995 Amazon already let customers post their own reviews of books, good or bad. In 1996 Pierre Omidyar opened the Feedback Forum on AuctionWeb, asking members to praise good trading partners and complain openly about bad ones. In 2002 a study by economists Paul Resnick and Richard Zeckhauser found that buyers left feedback about half the time, and that almost all of it was positive. In 2008 eBay stopped sellers from leaving negative feedback for buyers, after complaints of tit-for-tat revenge ratings.`,facts:[`More than 99 per cent of eBay feedback was positive, a 2002 study found.`,`eBay's feedback scores began in February 1996.`]},"tech:oneclick":{intro:`One-click ordering stores a customer's card number and delivery address after the first purchase, so later purchases need only a single click on a button. Every step removed from the checkout means fewer shoppers giving up halfway, and fewer second thoughts about buying.`,how:`The first time a customer orders, the shop keeps their payment and address details on its servers, linked to a cookie in their browser. When they return, a button beside each product places the order at once using the stored details, and the shop bundles orders placed close together into one parcel. The customer can still cancel for a short while, which makes the shortcut feel safe.`,history:`In 1997 Amazon filed for a US patent on one-click ordering, naming Jeff Bezos, Peri Hartman, Shel Kaphan and Joel Spiegel as inventors. In 1999 the patent was granted, and Amazon at once sued the bookseller Barnes & Noble over its Express Lane checkout. In 2000 publisher Tim O'Reilly led a protest by thousands of programmers against the patent. In 2000 Bezos replied with an open letter proposing that software patents should last only a few years. In 2017 the patent expired.`,facts:[`Bezos suggested in 2000 that software patents should last three to five years.`,`Amazon's one-click patent ran out in 2017, 20 years after it was filed.`]},"tech:itemrec":{intro:`Item-to-item recommendation suggests products by looking at what other shoppers did: people who bought this book also bought those. It needs no knowledge of what the books are about, only the shop's records of purchases, and the suggestions appear instantly on every product page.`,how:`Offline, the shop scans its order history and, for each pair of items, counts how many customers bought both, scoring how much more often they go together than chance would suggest. Each product gets a short list of its most similar items. When a shopper looks at a book or fills a basket, the site just looks up those lists and merges them, so it copes with millions of customers without comparing people with one another.`,history:`In 1998 Amazon engineers Greg Linden, Brent Smith and Jeremy York filed for a patent on item-to-item collaborative filtering. In 2001 the patent was granted. In 2003 the three described the method in the journal IEEE Internet Computing, explaining that it scaled to tens of millions of customers. In 2013 the consultancy McKinsey estimated that about 35 per cent of what customers bought on Amazon came from its recommendations. In 2017 IEEE Internet Computing gave the 2003 paper its first Test of Time award, marking the magazine's 20th year.`,facts:[`McKinsey estimated in 2013 that 35 per cent of Amazon's sales came from recommendations.`,`The 2003 paper described a catalogue of millions of items.`]},"tech:fulfilment":{intro:`A fulfilment centre is a huge warehouse run by an online shop to store goods close to its customers and pick, pack and ship orders within hours. Owning them costs a fortune, but it lets the shop hold stock itself instead of waiting on wholesalers, and promise fast, reliable delivery.`,how:`Goods arrive by the lorry-load and are shelved wherever there is room, often scattered at random, with a computer remembering each item's place. Pickers follow routes planned by the system to collect each order, conveyors and sorters bring the items together, and packers box them. Centres near big cities and deals with parcel carriers cut delivery from a week to a day or two.`,history:`In 1997 Amazon opened a second warehouse, in New Castle, Delaware, alongside its first in Seattle. In 1999 it spent heavily on new centres in places such as Fernley, Nevada, Coffeyville, Kansas, and Campbellsville, Kentucky. In 2001, with losses mounting after the dot-com crash, Amazon closed a centre in Georgia and cut about 1,300 jobs. In 2005 Amazon launched Prime, unlimited two-day shipping for a yearly fee. In 2006 it began storing and shipping goods for other sellers through Fulfillment by Amazon. In 2012 Amazon bought Kiva Systems, maker of robots that carry shelves to the pickers.`,facts:[`Amazon paid $775 million for Kiva Systems (2012).`,`Amazon cut about 1,300 jobs in January 2001.`]},"tech:p2ppay":{intro:`Email money lets anyone send a payment to anyone else who has an email address, without knowing their bank details. The service holds accounts for both people, moves money between them at once and pays out to a bank or card, which suits strangers trading on auction sites who would otherwise post cheques.`,how:`The sender links a card or bank account and types the recipient's email address and an amount. If the recipient has no account, they get an email inviting them to sign up and collect the money, so each payment recruits a new user. Moving money inside the service costs almost nothing; the company earns from fees on business accounts and from money resting in balances.`,history:`In 1998 Max Levchin and Peter Thiel founded Confinity, at first to beam money between Palm Pilot handheld computers. In 1999 Confinity launched PayPal, which sent money by email and paid each new user a sign-up bonus. In 2000 Confinity merged with X.com, Elon Musk's online bank. In 2001 the combined firm took the name PayPal. In 2002 PayPal went public, and eBay bought it later that year. In 2003 eBay closed Billpoint, its own payment service, which had lost out to PayPal.`,facts:[`PayPal paid $10 to each new user and $10 for each friend referred (1999).`,`eBay paid about $1.5 billion for PayPal (2002).`]},"tech:fraudml":{intro:`Machine-learned fraud detection studies thousands of past cases of stolen cards and fake accounts to learn the warning signs, then scores every new payment as it happens. Suspicious ones are held for a person to check before the money leaves, while honest customers pass straight through.`,how:`Every transaction is described by many features: the amount, the computer and network it came from, the age of the account, and how it links to other accounts. A model trained on past fraud gives each payment a risk score. Investigators use charts of linked accounts to spot rings of fraudsters, and their decisions feed back in as new training cases, because criminals change their tricks quickly.`,history:`In 1992 HNC Software in San Diego launched Falcon, which used neural networks to spot card fraud for banks. In 2000 PayPal's losses to fraud, much of it by organised gangs using stolen card numbers, threatened to sink the young company. In 2001 Max Levchin's team built IGOR, a system that flagged suspicious transfers for investigators. In 2003 Peter Thiel and colleagues founded Palantir to apply PayPal's methods of finding hidden links to intelligence work.`,facts:[`HNC launched its Falcon fraud detector for card issuers in 1992.`,`Palantir was founded in 2003, partly by former PayPal staff.`]},"tech:socialnet":{intro:`A social network site gives each member a profile page about themselves and a public list of friends who have also joined. Visitors browse from friend to friend, and on to friends of friends, finding people they know and people they might like to know: the web arranged around people rather than documents.`,how:`Members sign up, fill in a profile and invite their contacts by email, and each invitation pulls in more members. The site stores who is linked to whom and shows the connections, along with messages, photos and comments on each other's pages. Its value grows with every member, since there are more people to find, and it earns from adverts seen by members who come back day after day.`,history:`In 1997 Andrew Weinreich launched SixDegrees in New York, named after the idea that everyone is linked by six handshakes. In 2000 SixDegrees closed, too early for a world where few friends were online. In 2002 Jonathan Abrams launched Friendster in California, which grew so fast that its servers slowed to a crawl. In 2003 MySpace began in Los Angeles and became popular with bands and teenagers. In 2004 Mark Zuckerberg and fellow students launched TheFacebook at Harvard, open at first only to students at chosen universities. In 2006 Facebook opened to anyone aged 13 or over with an email address.`,facts:[`SixDegrees had about 3 million members before it closed in 2000.`,`News Corporation paid $580 million for MySpace's parent company (2005).`,`About half of Harvard's undergraduates joined TheFacebook in its first month (2004).`]},"tech:newsfeed":{intro:`A news feed collects everything a member's friends do, new photos, status updates, relationship changes, groups joined, and shows it on one page, newest and most interesting first. Instead of visiting friends' profiles one by one to see what has changed, members simply open the site and scroll.`,how:`Every action on the site creates a small story. For each member, the system gathers the stories from their friends and chooses which to show, weighing how close the member is to the friend, the type of story and how recent it is. The feed refreshes constantly, so there is always something new, and comments on a story bring friends back again.`,history:`In 2006 Facebook launched News Feed on 5 September, and within days hundreds of thousands of members joined groups protesting against it as an invasion of privacy. In 2006 Mark Zuckerberg apologised and added privacy controls, while the time people spent on the site went up. In 2009 Facebook added the Like button, a quick signal of which stories people enjoyed. In 2009 it began sorting the feed by default into Top News, chosen by popularity rather than time. In 2010 Facebook engineers described EdgeRank, the formula behind the ranking.`,facts:[`The largest protest group against News Feed gathered about 700,000 members (2006).`,`Facebook added the Like button in February 2009.`]},"tech:socialplatform":{intro:`A social app platform opens a social network to outside programmers. They can build games, quizzes and tools that run inside members' pages, use their friend lists with permission and post updates into friends' feeds, so a good app can spread through the network, friend to friend, in days.`,how:`The network publishes an interface that lets an app ask for a member's name, friends and other details once the member agrees, plus a markup language for drawing inside the site's pages. Apps run on the developers' own servers. Invitations and feed stories bring in new users for free, and developers earn from adverts and from selling virtual goods such as game items.`,history:`In 2007 Facebook opened its Platform to outside developers at its first f8 conference in San Francisco. In 2007 Microsoft paid $240 million for a small share of Facebook, valuing the company at $15 billion. In 2007 Google answered with OpenSocial, one shared interface for rival networks such as MySpace and its own Orkut. In 2008 Facebook cut back the invitations and notices apps could send, after members complained of spam. In 2010 Zynga's farming game FarmVille had more than 80 million players a month.`,facts:[`Microsoft paid $240 million for 1.6 per cent of Facebook (2007).`,`FarmVille had over 80 million monthly players in 2010.`]},"tech:microblog":{intro:`Microblogging means posting very short public messages, short enough to send as a phone text, that anyone can choose to follow. Instead of friends who must accept each other, people follow whoever interests them, and each person's page shows a running stream of the latest posts from everyone they follow.`,how:`Posts are limited to 140 characters, which leaves room for a username inside a 160-character text message, so people can post and receive from any mobile phone as well as on the web. Following is one-way, so one popular person can have huge numbers of followers. Users invent conventions of their own, such as starting a reply with @ and the person's name.`,history:`In 2006 Jack Dorsey, Biz Stone, Evan Williams and Noah Glass built Twitter as a side project inside Odeo, a podcasting start-up in San Francisco. In 2007 its use took off at the South by Southwest festival in Austin, Texas, where screens in the hallways showed the latest posts. In 2007 Chris Messina suggested marking topics with the # sign, the hashtag. In 2009 a passenger's photo of a US Airways jet floating in the Hudson River spread on Twitter before the television news had it. In 2017 Twitter doubled its limit to 280 characters.`,facts:[`Daily posts at South by Southwest rose from about 20,000 to 60,000 (2007).`,`Twitter's limit rose from 140 to 280 characters in 2017.`]},"tech:videoshare":{intro:`Web video sharing lets anyone upload a video clip to a website, which converts it and plays it straight in the browser, with no special player to download and no wait for the whole file. Each clip gets its own page and a snippet of code that lets people show it on their own blogs and profiles.`,how:`Uploaded videos, in whatever format, are converted on the site's servers into Flash video, which plays through the Flash plug-in already found in almost every browser. Playback starts while the rest is still arriving. Viewers rate, comment and find related clips, and embedded copies on blogs and profile pages spread the videos, while the site pays for huge amounts of bandwidth.`,history:`In 2005 Chad Hurley, Steve Chen and Jawed Karim, who had worked at PayPal, founded YouTube in California, and Karim uploaded its first clip, a short video at a zoo, in April. In 2005 a Nike advert featuring the footballer Ronaldinho became one of the first clips to pass a million views. In 2006 Google bought YouTube. In 2007 YouTube began sharing advertising money with popular uploaders through its Partner Program. In 2010 YouTube passed 2 billion views a day.`,facts:[`Google paid $1.65 billion in shares for YouTube (2006).`,`YouTube passed 2 billion views a day in 2010.`]},"tech:dvdmail":{intro:`Rentals by mail let customers choose films on a website and receive DVDs in the post, with a prepaid envelope to send them back. A DVD is light and thin enough to post cheaply, and a website can offer far more titles than the shelves of any local video shop.`,how:`Subscribers pay a fixed monthly fee and keep a queue of films they want to see. The service posts the next few, the customer keeps them as long as they like with no late fees, and each returned disc sends out the next one in the queue. Warehouses within a day's post of most homes keep the round trip short, and a recommendation engine steers people towards older films that are in stock.`,history:`In 1997 Reed Hastings and Marc Randolph founded Netflix in Scotts Valley, California, after testing whether a disc would survive the post by mailing a CD to Hastings' home. In 1998 the site opened, renting each DVD separately. In 1999 it switched to a monthly subscription. In 2000 the video chain Blockbuster turned down a chance to buy Netflix for $50 million. In 2007 Netflix delivered its billionth DVD. In 2010 Blockbuster filed for bankruptcy.`,facts:[`Netflix opened in 1998 with about 925 titles, nearly every DVD then in print.`,`Netflix delivered its billionth DVD in 2007.`]},"tech:vodstream":{intro:`A streaming subscription lets members press play on a film or a television episode and watch it at once over the internet, with no disc and no download to wait for, as part of a monthly fee. It needs broadband fast enough to carry video smoothly, and studios willing to license their films to be shown this way.`,how:`Each title is stored on the service's servers in several versions at different quality levels. The player measures the connection as it goes and switches to a lower or higher version so playback never stalls. Copies are spread across servers close to the viewers. Licences from the studios limit what can be offered and for how long, so the catalogue keeps changing.`,history:`In 2007 Netflix added Watch Now to its subscriptions, streaming films in a web browser on Windows PCs. In 2007 the BBC's iPlayer began streaming its programmes free to UK viewers on Christmas Day. In 2008 Hulu, owned by NBC and News Corporation, opened to the public, and Netflix streams reached television sets through Roku's small box and the Xbox 360. In 2011 Netflix lost about 800,000 US subscribers after raising prices and announcing a plan to split off its DVD business. In 2013 Netflix released House of Cards, a drama series made for it.`,facts:[`Netflix lost about 800,000 US subscribers in three months of 2011.`,`BBC iPlayer began streaming on 25 December 2007.`]},"tech:ajaxapps":{intro:`A rich web app uses JavaScript in the page to fetch data from the server in the background and update parts of the screen without reloading the whole page. Maps can be dragged smoothly, mail opens instantly and forms check themselves as you type, so a website starts to feel like a program installed on the computer.`,how:`The key is a browser object called XMLHttpRequest, which lets a script ask the server for data quietly while the user carries on. The script then rewrites part of the page through the Document Object Model, the browser's live map of the page's parts. Because each request moves only a little data, the app feels fast on ordinary broadband. The name Ajax stands for Asynchronous JavaScript and XML.`,history:`In 1999 Microsoft added XMLHTTP to Internet Explorer 5 so the web version of its Outlook email could fetch messages in the background. In 2004 Google launched Gmail, which used the technique to feel like a desktop mail program. In 2005 Google Maps let users drag a map smoothly, fetching new map tiles around the edges as they went. In 2005 Jesse James Garrett of the design firm Adaptive Path named the approach Ajax. In 2006 John Resig released jQuery, which made such scripts much easier to write for every browser.`,facts:[`Google Maps launched in February 2005.`,`jQuery was first released in August 2006.`]},"tech:freemium":{intro:`Freemium gives a product away free to everyone and charges for a premium version with more space, more features or no adverts. Most users never pay, but serving them costs little in software, and the few who do pay can fund the rest, while the free users spread the word.`,how:`The free tier must be good enough to attract and keep millions of users, and the paid tier clearly better for the keen ones: more storage, business features or extra content. Often only a few per cent upgrade. Because each extra user costs almost nothing to serve, the company grows by word of mouth instead of a sales team, and tunes its offer by measuring what makes people pay.`,history:`In 1982 Andrew Fluegelman released PC-Talk for the IBM PC as freeware, asking users who liked it to send him a payment. In 1993 id Software gave away the first episode of its game Doom and sold the rest. In 2004 Skype, whose computer-to-computer calls were free, began charging for calls to ordinary phones. In 2006 venture capitalist Fred Wilson described the model on his blog and readers helped give it its name. In 2008 Dropbox launched with free storage and paid plans for more. In 2009 Chris Anderson's book Free argued that this was how much of the web would make money.`,facts:[`Fluegelman asked satisfied PC-Talk users for $25 (1982).`,`Dropbox offered 2 GB free and 50 GB for $9.99 a month (2008).`]},"tech:appstore":{intro:`An app store is one shop built into every phone, where users browse, buy and install programs with a tap, paying through the account they already have. Developers anywhere can sell to millions of phone owners without deals with phone networks or shops, in return for a share of each sale.`,how:`Developers write apps with the phone maker's tools, submit them for review and set a price, often under a dollar, or free. The store handles hosting, payment, updates and refunds and keeps 30 per cent of the price. Charts and featured lists decide which apps get noticed. Each app runs in its own protected space on the phone, so a bad one cannot easily harm the rest.`,history:`In 2008 Apple released its kit for writing iPhone software in March and opened the App Store in July. In 2008 Google opened Android Market for phones running its Android system in October. In 2009 the App Store passed a billion downloads in April. In 2009 Apple's television adverts made the line "There's an app for that" a catchphrase. In 2010 the App Store passed 250,000 apps.`,facts:[`Apple's store kept 30 per cent of each sale (2008).`,`The App Store passed 1 billion downloads in April 2009.`]},"tech:neurons":{intro:`An artificial neuron is a simple mathematical model of a brain cell. It adds up signals from its inputs, some exciting it and some holding it back, and fires only if the total reaches a threshold. Wired together, such switches can carry out any rule of logic, which suggests that a brain might be understood as a kind of calculating machine.`,how:`Each model neuron is either on or off. Its inputs come from other neurons, and if enough exciting inputs are on at once, with no inhibiting one, it switches on at the next tick of time. With one threshold a neuron acts as AND, with another as OR, and chains of them can count, remember and compute whatever a logic circuit can. The connections are fixed by the designer: the model does not learn.`,history:`In 1943 the neurophysiologist Warren McCulloch and the young logician Walter Pitts, working in Chicago, published their logical model of the neuron. In 1945 John von Neumann used their neuron notation to describe the logic of the EDVAC computer design. In 1949 the Canadian psychologist Donald Hebb proposed that a connection between two neurons grows stronger when they fire together, a rule for learning. In 1951 Marvin Minsky and Dean Edmonds built SNARC, a learning machine of 40 artificial neurons made from valves and motors. In 1959 Pitts, McCulloch and colleagues published "What the Frog's Eye Tells the Frog's Brain", on how real nerve cells pick out features.`,facts:[`Walter Pitts was about 20 when the 1943 paper appeared.`,`SNARC simulated 40 neurons using about 3,000 vacuum tubes (1951).`]},"tech:imitation":{intro:`The imitation game is a test for machine intelligence. A judge holds typed conversations with two hidden players, one a person and one a computer, and tries to tell which is which. If the machine fools the judge as often as a person would, the argument goes, there is no fair reason to deny that it thinks.`,how:`The questions can be about anything: poems, chess, sums, feelings. Answers travel by teleprinter so that voice and looks give nothing away. The test sidesteps arguments about what thinking is and asks only about behaviour that can be checked. Alan Turing answers the objections one by one, from theology to the claim that machines only do what they are told, and suggests that a learning child machine may be easier to build than an adult mind.`,history:`In 1950 Alan Turing, then at the University of Manchester, published "Computing Machinery and Intelligence" in the philosophy journal Mind. In 1950 he predicted that within about fifty years a computer would play the game well enough to fool an average judge for five minutes three times in ten. In 1952 Turing debated whether machines could think on BBC radio. In 1991 the first Loebner Prize contest, paid for by the American businessman Hugh Loebner, put chat programs in front of judges. In 2014 a chatbot posing as a 13-year-old Ukrainian boy, Eugene Goostman, convinced a third of the judges at a Royal Society event in London.`,facts:[`Turing guessed in 1950 that a machine with about a billion bits of storage could play well.`,`Eugene Goostman fooled 33 per cent of judges in five-minute chats (2014).`]},"tech:perceptron":{intro:`The perceptron is a machine that learns to recognise patterns from examples. Shown a picture, it adds up signals from its light sensors with adjustable weights and answers yes or no; when it answers wrongly, it shifts its weights a little towards the right answer, and over many examples it learns to sort shapes or letters by itself.`,how:`Light falls on a grid of photocells. Each cell connects at random to association units, whose outputs feed a response unit through weights. If the weighted sum passes a threshold, the answer is yes. After each wrong answer, the learning rule strengthens the inputs that should have pushed towards the right answer and weakens those that pushed the wrong way. In the Mark I machine the weights are potentiometers turned by electric motors as it learns.`,history:`In 1957 Frank Rosenblatt at the Cornell Aeronautical Laboratory in Buffalo, New York, described the perceptron in a report, with money from the US Navy. In 1958 he demonstrated a simulated perceptron on an IBM 704, and the New York Times reported the Navy's hope of a machine that could one day walk, talk and know it existed. In 1960 the Mark I Perceptron, a custom-built machine, was working. In 1962 Rosenblatt's book Principles of Neurodynamics set out his theory. In 1969 Marvin Minsky and Seymour Papert's book Perceptrons proved that a single layer could not learn some simple patterns, and money for neural network research dried up. In 1971 Rosenblatt died in a boating accident.`,facts:[`The Mark I Perceptron's eye was a 20 by 20 grid of photocells (1960).`,`Perceptrons (1969) showed one layer cannot learn exclusive-or: one input or the other, but not both.`]},"tech:hopfield":{intro:`A Hopfield network is a web of artificial neurons, each connected to all the others, that stores memories as stable patterns. Give it a damaged or partial pattern and it settles step by step into the nearest stored one, the way a ball rolls into the nearest valley. The Boltzmann machine adds randomness and a way to learn hidden structure in data.`,how:`Every pair of neurons has a two-way connection weight, set so that the stored patterns are low points of an energy that can only fall as neurons flip one at a time. A partial picture starts high on a slope and rolls down to a complete memory. The Boltzmann machine, borrowing from physics, lets neurons flip at random under a temperature that is slowly lowered, and has hidden units that learn the statistics of the examples shown to it.`,history:`In 1982 John Hopfield, a physicist at Caltech, published his network in the Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences, linking neural networks to the physics of magnetic materials called spin glasses. In 1985 Geoffrey Hinton, Terry Sejnowski and David Ackley published a learning rule for the Boltzmann machine. In 1985 Hopfield and David Tank showed a network finding short routes for the travelling salesman problem. In 1985 the papers helped bring physicists and engineers back to neural networks after years of neglect. In 2024 Hopfield and Hinton shared the Nobel Prize in Physics for this work.`,facts:[`Hopfield estimated in 1982 that a network could hold about 0.15 memories per neuron.`,`Hopfield and Hinton shared the 2024 Nobel Prize in Physics.`]},"tech:backprop":{intro:`Backpropagation is a way to train a neural network that has hidden layers between its input and output. After each example, the error at the output is sent backwards through the network, and calculus tells every connection how much it contributed to the mistake and which way to change. Networks trained like this can invent their own inner features.`,how:`The network makes a guess by passing signals forward, layer by layer, through smooth S-shaped units. The error, the difference from the right answer, is then passed backwards using the chain rule of calculus, giving each weight its share of the blame. Every weight is nudged a little downhill, and after many passes over the examples the hidden units come to stand for useful features that nobody programmed in.`,history:`In 1970 the Finnish student Seppo Linnainmaa described the reverse mode of automatic differentiation, the mathematics behind the method. In 1974 Paul Werbos proposed using it to train networks in his Harvard PhD thesis. In 1986 David Rumelhart, Geoffrey Hinton and Ronald Williams showed in Nature that it let networks learn useful internal representations. In 1986 the two-volume book Parallel Distributed Processing, edited by Rumelhart and James McClelland, spread the method widely. In 1987 Terry Sejnowski and Charles Rosenberg reported NETtalk, a network that learned to read English text aloud.`,facts:[`NETtalk had about 300 units and 18,000 connections (1987).`,`The 1986 Nature paper by Rumelhart, Hinton and Williams was four pages long.`]},"tech:convnets":{intro:`A convolutional network is a neural network built for pictures. Instead of connecting every pixel to every neuron, it slides the same small feature detector across the whole image, so an edge or a curve is recognised wherever it appears. Layers build up from edges to strokes to whole shapes, with far fewer weights to learn.`,how:`Each layer holds several small filters, grids of weights a few pixels wide. Each filter is applied at every position of the image, producing a map of where its feature appears; pooling then shrinks the map so small shifts do not matter. Deeper layers combine the features found below them. The whole stack is trained with backpropagation on labelled examples, such as thousands of handwritten digits.`,history:`In 1962 David Hubel and Torsten Wiesel described cells in a cat's visual cortex that respond to edges at particular angles. In 1980 Kunihiko Fukushima in Japan described the neocognitron, a layered network for recognising shapes inspired by that work. In 1989 Yann LeCun and colleagues at Bell Labs trained a convolutional network with backpropagation to read handwritten US postcodes. In 1998 LeCun, Léon Bottou, Yoshua Bengio and Patrick Haffner described LeNet-5 and the MNIST set of handwritten digits. In 2012 AlexNet, a much larger convolutional network, won the ImageNet contest by a wide margin.`,facts:[`LeNet-5 had about 60,000 trainable weights (1998).`,`MNIST holds 60,000 training and 10,000 test digits (1998).`,`AlexNet cut the ImageNet top-5 error from about 26 to 15 per cent (2012).`]},"tech:lstm":{intro:`Long short-term memory is a kind of recurrent neural network, one that reads a sequence step by step, built to remember things for a long time. Each unit has a memory cell and gates that learn when to store new information, when to keep it and when to let it out, so the network can link a word to another seen hundreds of steps before.`,how:`Ordinary recurrent networks pass their state from step to step, but when trained by backpropagation through time the error signal shrinks or explodes as it travels back, so they cannot learn long gaps. The LSTM memory cell carries its contents forward unchanged unless a gate says otherwise, so the error flows back intact. The gates are small neurons themselves, trained along with everything else.`,history:`In 1991 Sepp Hochreiter, a student of Jürgen Schmidhuber in Munich, showed in his diploma thesis why recurrent networks forget: the vanishing gradient. In 1997 Hochreiter and Schmidhuber published LSTM in the journal Neural Computation. In 2000 Felix Gers, Schmidhuber and Fred Cummins added a forget gate, letting the cell clear its memory. In 2009 LSTM networks built by Alex Graves won international handwriting recognition contests. In 2016 Google switched its Translate service to a neural system built from LSTM layers.`,facts:[`Hochreiter's 1991 thesis was written in German at the Technical University of Munich.`,`Google's 2016 translation system stacked 8 LSTM layers on each side.`]},"tech:dbn":{intro:`A deep belief net is a neural network with many layers that is trained one layer at a time. Each layer learns, without labels, to describe the patterns in the layer below it; then the whole stack is fine-tuned on the real task. The trick makes deep networks trainable at last, and gives the field a new name: deep learning.`,how:`Each pair of layers forms a restricted Boltzmann machine, a simple network that learns to rebuild its input through a layer of hidden features. Once the first layer has learned features of the raw pixels, its outputs become the training data for the next layer, and so on up. This greedy, layer-by-layer start puts the weights in a good region, and backpropagation then fine-tunes the whole network.`,history:`In 2002 Geoffrey Hinton published contrastive divergence, a quick way to train restricted Boltzmann machines. In 2004 the Canadian Institute for Advanced Research began funding Hinton's programme on neural computation, keeping a small research community together. In 2006 Hinton, Simon Osindero and Yee-Whye Teh published a fast learning method for deep belief nets, and Hinton and Ruslan Salakhutdinov showed in Science that deep networks could compress images and text. In 2006 Yoshua Bengio's group in Montreal showed that layer-by-layer training also worked with stacked autoencoders. In 2009 Hinton's students Abdel-rahman Mohamed and George Dahl applied deep belief nets to speech recognition.`,facts:[`Hinton's 2006 deep belief net misread only about 1.25 per cent of MNIST digits.`,`Its hidden layers had 500, 500 and 2,000 units (2006).`]},"tech:gpunets":{intro:`Training neural networks on graphics cards uses chips built to colour millions of pixels at once for the multiply-and-add sums that neural networks are made of. A single gaming card can train a network tens of times faster than an ordinary processor, so experiments that took weeks finish in a day, and much bigger networks become possible.`,how:`A neural network's work is mostly multiplying large grids of numbers, matrices, and each entry can be computed separately. A graphics processor has hundreds of small cores and very fast memory, built to run the same instruction on many pieces of data at once. Programmers write the network's sums as kernels in CUDA, NVIDIA's programming system, and keep the weights and training data in the card's own memory to avoid slow copying.`,history:`In 2005 Dave Steinkraus, Patrice Simard and Ian Buck showed that graphics cards could speed up neural network training. In 2006 Kumar Chellapilla and colleagues at Microsoft ran convolutional networks for document reading on a graphics card several times faster than on a processor. In 2009 Rajat Raina, Anand Madhavan and Andrew Ng at Stanford trained large deep belief nets on graphics cards. In 2010 Dan Ciresan and colleagues in Jürgen Schmidhuber's lab in Switzerland set a record on handwritten digits with a plain deep network trained on a graphics card. In 2012 AlexNet, trained on two NVIDIA cards, won the ImageNet contest.`,facts:[`Ciresan's 2010 network made only 0.35 per cent errors on handwritten digits.`,`AlexNet trained for about six days on two GTX 580 graphics cards (2012).`]},"tech:imagenet":{intro:`A big labelled dataset is a huge collection of examples, here photographs, each tagged by people with what it shows. With millions of pictures in thousands of categories, from dog breeds to kinds of mushroom, it gives vision programs enough examples to learn from, and a common test on which to compare them.`,how:`Images are gathered by searching the web for every noun in WordNet, a dictionary that arranges English words into a tree of meanings. Workers on Amazon's Mechanical Turk, paid a little for each task, check whether each image really shows its word, and several workers vote on every picture to catch mistakes. A yearly contest then scores programs on a set of test images kept back from training.`,history:`In 2006 Fei-Fei Li, then at the University of Illinois, began planning a dataset that would map out the whole world of objects. In 2007 Li moved to Princeton, where her team began collecting images and paying online workers to label them. In 2009 the team presented ImageNet at the CVPR conference in Miami. In 2010 the first ImageNet Large Scale Visual Recognition Challenge asked programs to sort images into 1,000 categories. In 2012 AlexNet, a deep convolutional network, won the challenge by a huge margin. In 2015 a Microsoft network, ResNet, scored better than the estimated human error rate.`,facts:[`The challenge used about 1.2 million training images in 1,000 classes (2010).`,`Microsoft's ResNet scored 3.6 per cent top-5 error in 2015.`]},"tech:distbelief":{intro:`Distributed training splits the work of training one giant neural network across many computers. The network itself can be cut into pieces held on different machines, and many copies can learn from different slices of the data at once, sharing what they learn through central servers that keep the master copy of the weights.`,how:`In model parallelism each machine holds part of the network and passes signals to its neighbours. In data parallelism many replicas each train on their own share of the examples and send updates to parameter servers, which combine them and hand back fresh weights. The replicas do not wait for each other, so a slow machine holds nobody up, and thousands of ordinary processors can work on one network.`,history:`In 2011 Andrew Ng, Jeff Dean and Greg Corrado started the Google Brain project to train very large networks in Google's data centres. In 2012 the team trained a network with a billion connections on frames from YouTube videos, and without being told what to look for it learned to detect cats. In 2012 Dean and colleagues described the training system, DistBelief, at the NIPS conference. In 2012 Google used such networks to improve speech recognition on Android phones. In 2015 Google released TensorFlow, the successor to DistBelief, as free open-source software.`,facts:[`Google's 2012 network learned from 10 million frames of YouTube video.`,`TensorFlow, DistBelief's successor, was released free in November 2015.`]},"tech:heuristic":{intro:`Heuristic search is a way for a program to solve problems that have too many possibilities to try them all. Like a person, it uses rules of thumb to try the most promising steps first and to drop the hopeless ones, giving up the guarantee of the best answer in return for finding a good one in reasonable time.`,how:`The problem is pictured as a tree: a starting state, the moves possible from it, the moves from each of those, and so on. A blind search would explore it all; a heuristic program scores each state by how close it seems to the goal and expands the best first. The Logic Theorist works backwards from the theorem to be proved, looking for axioms or proven results that could lead to it.`,history:`In 1955 John McCarthy, Marvin Minsky, Nathaniel Rochester and Claude Shannon proposed a summer workshop at Dartmouth College on "artificial intelligence", coining the term. In 1956 Allen Newell, Cliff Shaw and Herbert Simon of RAND and Carnegie Tech brought the Logic Theorist to that workshop, often called the first artificial intelligence program. In 1957 the same team began the General Problem Solver, which worked by shrinking the difference between where it was and where it wanted to be. In 1958 Simon and Newell predicted that a computer would be world chess champion within ten years. In 1975 Newell and Simon shared the Turing Award.`,facts:[`The Logic Theorist found a shorter proof of one theorem than Russell and Whitehead had (1956).`,`Newell and Simon shared the 1975 Turing Award.`]},"tech:gamesearch":{intro:`Game-tree search lets a computer play board games by looking ahead: it imagines each possible move, each reply and each reply to the reply, and scores the positions at the end. Assuming both sides play their best, it picks the move that leads to the best result it can force. Pruning skips lines of play that cannot change the decision.`,how:`The program builds a tree of moves to a fixed depth and scores each final position with an evaluation function: in draughts, pieces, kings and control of the centre. The minimax rule passes scores back up the tree, taking the best for the side to move at each level. Alpha-beta pruning stops exploring a move as soon as it is clearly worse than one already found, letting the program search much deeper in the same time.`,history:`In 1950 Claude Shannon published "Programming a Computer for Playing Chess", setting out minimax search with an evaluation function. In 1952 Arthur Samuel wrote his first checkers program, as Americans call draughts, for the IBM 701. In 1959 Samuel described how his program improved its own evaluation by playing, calling it machine learning. In 1962 Samuel's program beat Robert Nealey, a Connecticut player, in a single game. In 1975 Donald Knuth and Ronald Moore published a full analysis of alpha-beta pruning.`,facts:[`Shannon estimated chess has about 10 to the power 120 possible games (1950).`,`Samuel's program beat Robert Nealey in one game in 1962.`]},"tech:chatterbot":{intro:`A pattern-matching chatbot holds a typed conversation by spotting keywords in what the user says and reflecting them back according to a script. It understands nothing, yet a well-chosen script, such as a psychotherapist who answers questions with questions, can make people feel listened to and even confide in it.`,how:`ELIZA looks for keywords in each sentence, ranks them and applies a matching rule that splits the sentence around them; a reassembly rule then builds a reply, swapping I for you and my for your. If nothing matches, it falls back on stock lines such as "Please go on", or brings up an earlier topic. The DOCTOR script plays a therapist because a therapist can plausibly seem to know nothing about the world.`,history:`In 1966 Joseph Weizenbaum at MIT published ELIZA, which ran on the time-sharing computers of Project MAC. In 1972 the psychiatrist Kenneth Colby's PARRY, a program that imitated a paranoid patient, held a conversation with ELIZA over the ARPANET. In 1976 Weizenbaum's book Computer Power and Human Reason warned against trusting machines with human judgement, recalling that his secretary had asked him to leave the room so she could talk to ELIZA alone. In 1995 Richard Wallace began ALICE, a pattern-matching chatbot with thousands of rules. In 2004 ALICE won the Loebner Prize for the third time.`,facts:[`The ELIZA paper appeared in Communications of the ACM in January 1966.`,`ALICE won the Loebner Prize in 2000, 2001 and 2004.`]},"tech:planning":{intro:`Automated planning lets a program work out a sequence of actions that reaches a goal: a route across a room, or the steps to push a box through a doorway. A* search finds the shortest path by always exploring the step that looks best overall; STRIPS describes actions by what must be true before them and what they change.`,how:`A* keeps a list of partial routes and always extends the one with the lowest total of distance already travelled plus an estimate of the distance left. If the estimate never overstates the true distance, as a straight line never does, the first complete route found is the shortest. STRIPS lists each action's preconditions, the facts it adds and the facts it removes, and searches for a chain of actions that turns the starting facts into the goal.`,history:`In 1966 the Stanford Research Institute began building Shakey, a wheeled robot with a television camera and a range finder, linked by radio to a large computer. In 1968 Peter Hart, Nils Nilsson and Bertram Raphael published A*, first worked out to plan Shakey's routes. In 1970 Life magazine called Shakey the first electronic person. In 1971 Richard Fikes and Nils Nilsson described STRIPS, which Shakey used to plan sequences of moves. In 2004 Shakey was named to Carnegie Mellon University's Robot Hall of Fame.`,facts:[`The name A* came from earlier versions called A1 and A2 (1968).`,`Shakey joined the Robot Hall of Fame in 2004.`]},"tech:blocksworld":{intro:`Micro-world understanding means building a program that really understands language, but only about a tiny, simplified world. SHRDLU knows a table top of coloured blocks, pyramids and a box; it follows commands in ordinary English, answers questions about what it has done and why, and asks what you mean when a sentence is unclear.`,how:`The program combines a parser for English grammar, a reasoning system that plans block moves and a model of the scene, written in Lisp and a planning language called Micro-Planner. Told to find a block taller than the one it holds and put it in the box, it works out which objects the words refer to, plans the moves, including clearing other blocks off the top, and remembers its actions so it can explain why it did something.`,history:`In 1968 Terry Winograd began SHRDLU as a PhD student at MIT. In 1970 Patrick Winston, also at MIT, wrote a program that learned concepts such as an arch from examples built in a blocks world. In 1971 Winograd finished his thesis on SHRDLU. In 1972 it appeared as the book Understanding Natural Language and impressed researchers worldwide. In 1986 Winograd and Fernando Flores argued in their book Understanding Computers and Cognition that such programs could never grow into real understanding.`,facts:[`SHRDLU took its name from ETAOIN SHRDLU, the order of keys on a Linotype typesetter (1971).`,`Winograd's thesis was published as a book in 1972.`]},"tech:logicprog":{intro:`Logic programming lets a programmer write down facts and rules in a form of logic and then ask questions; the computer searches for a proof, and the proof is the answer. Instead of telling the machine how to compute something step by step, the program says what is true, which suits language, databases and puzzles.`,how:`A Prolog program is a list of clauses, such as: X is a grandparent of Z if X is a parent of Y and Y is a parent of Z. A question is matched against the heads of the clauses by unification, a pattern match that fills in variables, and the conditions become new questions, tried in order. If one fails, the system backtracks and tries the next clause, so it can find every answer in turn.`,history:`In 1965 Alan Robinson published resolution, a single rule of reasoning that a computer could use to prove theorems. In 1972 Alain Colmerauer and Philippe Roussel in Marseille created Prolog, at first to understand French sentences, drawing on ideas from Robert Kowalski in Edinburgh. In 1974 Kowalski published "Predicate Logic as Programming Language", explaining how a proof could be read as a computation. In 1977 David Warren's compiler for DEC-10 Prolog in Edinburgh made the language fast enough for serious use. In 1982 Japan's Fifth Generation Computer Systems project chose logic programming as its core.`,facts:[`Prolog's name comes from programmation en logique, French for programming in logic (1972).`,`Japan's Fifth Generation project ran from 1982 to 1992.`]},"tech:rulebase":{intro:`An expert system captures a human specialist's know-how as hundreds of if-then rules and uses them to give advice in one narrow field: which infection a patient has and which antibiotic to give, or which molecule a chemical analysis shows. It can explain its reasoning by showing the rules it used.`,how:`Knowledge engineers interview the expert and write each piece of judgement as a rule with a certainty factor: if the organism is rod-shaped and stains a certain way, then there is some evidence it is a particular bacterium. An inference engine chains through the rules, asking the user questions when it needs facts, and combines the certainties. Asked why, it shows which rule it is trying; asked how, the rules that led to a conclusion.`,history:`In 1965 Edward Feigenbaum, Joshua Lederberg and Bruce Buchanan at Stanford began DENDRAL, which worked out the structure of molecules from mass spectrometer readings. In 1972 Edward Shortliffe began MYCIN at Stanford as his doctoral work, to advise doctors on blood infections. In 1976 Shortliffe's book on MYCIN described its rules, certainty factors and explanations. In 1977 Feigenbaum coined the term knowledge engineering for the craft of drawing rules out of experts. In 1979 a study in the Journal of the American Medical Association found MYCIN's treatment choices at least as good as those of Stanford's infection specialists.`,facts:[`MYCIN's name comes from the ending shared by many antibiotics (1972).`,`Feigenbaum coined knowledge engineering in 1977.`]},"tech:eshell":{intro:`An expert-system shell is an expert system with the knowledge taken out: the inference engine, the rule language, the explanations and the user interface, ready to be filled with rules for any new field. Companies can build their own advisers without writing the reasoning machinery from scratch.`,how:`The developer writes rules in the shell's language and describes the objects of the field, such as parts, customer orders or symptoms. The shell's engine matches rules against the facts in its working memory and fires those that apply, either forwards from facts to conclusions or backwards from a goal to the facts it needs. Tools for editing, tracing and testing rules come built in.`,history:`In 1979 William van Melle at Stanford built EMYCIN, short for empty MYCIN, which kept MYCIN's engine and threw away its medical rules. In 1980 Digital Equipment Corporation began using XCON, written by John McDermott of Carnegie Mellon in the OPS5 rule language, to check and complete orders for its VAX computers. In 1981 Stanford researchers founded Teknowledge to sell expert systems to companies. In 1983 IntelliCorp released KEE, a shell for Lisp machines. In 1985 NASA's Johnson Space Center began building CLIPS, a shell written in C so it could run on ordinary computers.`,facts:[`XCON was first called R1 when it was being built (1978).`,`NASA started work on CLIPS in 1985.`]},"tech:lispmachine":{intro:`A Lisp machine is a personal workstation whose processor and operating system are built to run Lisp, the favourite language of artificial intelligence research. It gives one programmer a large memory, a big bit-mapped screen and a whole environment, from editor to windows, written in Lisp and open to change.`,how:`Lisp programs are made of lists linked by pointers, and they create and discard data constantly. The Lisp machine tags every word in memory with its type, so the hardware checks types and follows pointers quickly, and it collects garbage, reusing memory no longer in use, while programs run. Programmers can inspect and change any part of the running system, even the operating system itself, while it is working.`,history:`In 1974 Richard Greenblatt and Tom Knight at MIT's AI Laboratory began designing a computer for Lisp. In 1979 Greenblatt founded Lisp Machines Inc to sell the improved CADR design. In 1980 other MIT researchers founded a rival firm, Symbolics. In 1981 Symbolics shipped its LM-2. In 1985 Symbolics registered symbolics.com, the first .com address on the Internet. In 1987 the market collapsed as cheaper Unix workstations from firms such as Sun ran Lisp almost as well.`,facts:[`Symbolics registered the first .com domain name on 15 March 1985.`,`Texas Instruments and Xerox also sold Lisp workstations by 1984.`]},"tech:cyc":{intro:`A common-sense knowledge base tries to give computers the everyday knowledge that every child has and no program does: that water is wet, that people cannot be in two places at once, that a dead person stays dead. The Cyc project sets out to type millions of such facts and rules into one huge logical database by hand.`,how:`Facts are written in CycL, a formal logic language, and arranged in an ontology: a vast tree of concepts running from "thing" down to particular objects, with rules about how they relate. Knowledge is split into microtheories, contexts such as physics or fiction in which different assumptions hold. An inference engine combines the facts to answer questions and spot contradictions, and teams of trained ontologists spend years adding entries.`,history:`In 1976 Douglas Lenat's Automated Mathematician program at Stanford rediscovered ideas such as prime numbers, then ran out of steam for lack of background knowledge. In 1984 Lenat began Cyc at the Microelectronics and Computer Technology Corporation in Austin, Texas, a research group set up by US companies in answer to Japan's Fifth Generation project. In 1990 Lenat and R V Guha described the project in the book Building Large Knowledge-Based Systems. In 1994 the project moved into its own company, Cycorp. In 2002 Cycorp released OpenCyc, a free version of part of the knowledge base.`,facts:[`Lenat's Automated Mathematician rediscovered prime numbers in 1976.`,`OpenCyc, a free subset of Cyc, was released in 2002.`]},"tech:rulemt":{intro:`Rule-based machine translation turns text from one language into another with a bilingual dictionary and grammar rules written by linguists. It copes tolerably with narrow, repetitive texts such as technical manuals and weather reports, and comically with idioms, puns and poetry, because the rules know nothing of meaning.`,how:`The program looks up each word, uses rules to choose between meanings by looking at nearby words, and applies more rules to reorder the words and change their endings for the target language. More ambitious systems first analyse whole sentences into grammatical trees and then transfer the trees. Every new pair of languages and every exception means more hand-written rules and bigger dictionaries.`,history:`In 1949 Warren Weaver of the Rockefeller Foundation circulated a memo suggesting that computers could translate, treating a foreign text as a kind of code. In 1954 Georgetown University and IBM showed a program turning more than 60 Russian sentences into English on an IBM 701 in New York. In 1966 the US government's ALPAC report found machine translation slower, less accurate and dearer than human translation, and funding was cut. In 1968 Peter Toma founded Systran, whose Russian-English system the US Air Force adopted. In 1976 the European Commission began using Systran to help translate its documents.`,facts:[`The Georgetown-IBM demonstration took place in January 1954.`,`After the 1966 ALPAC report, US funding for machine translation almost stopped.`]},"tech:omnifont":{intro:`Omni-font optical character recognition reads printed text in almost any typeface, not only the special fonts that earlier reading machines need. It looks for the features that make a letter that letter, loops, strokes and gaps, so it can cope with books, magazines and typed letters as they are, and turn them into text a computer can store or read aloud.`,how:`A flatbed scanner captures the page as a grid of dots. The program finds the lines and separates each character, then describes its shape by features such as closed loops, line ends, junctions and hollows, rather than comparing it dot by dot with stored templates. Context settles doubtful cases, such as a letter O against a zero. A speech synthesiser can then read the text aloud.`,history:`In 1968 machines began reading OCR-A, a typeface designed in the US to be easy for machines to read, because reading machines needed such special fonts. In 1974 Ray Kurzweil founded Kurzweil Computer Products in Massachusetts to build a machine that could read any print. In 1976 he unveiled the Kurzweil Reading Machine, which read books aloud to blind people using a flatbed scanner, omni-font recognition and a speech synthesiser. In 1976 the musician Stevie Wonder bought one of the first machines. In 1980 Xerox bought Kurzweil Computer Products.`,facts:[`Stevie Wonder bought one of the first Kurzweil reading machines in 1976.`,`Xerox bought Kurzweil Computer Products in 1980.`]},"tech:hmm":{intro:`A hidden Markov model treats speech as a chain of hidden states, the sounds a speaker means to make, which are never seen directly, only the noisy sound signals they produce. Using probabilities learned from many recordings, a recogniser works out the most likely chain of words behind the sound, instead of matching stored templates.`,how:`Each word is built from states for its sounds, with probabilities for moving from one state to the next and for each state producing each kind of sound frame, a snapshot of the frequencies taken a hundred times a second. A language model gives the probability of each word following the ones before. The Viterbi algorithm searches efficiently for the likeliest path through all of them, and training on recordings tunes the probabilities automatically.`,history:`In 1966 Leonard Baum and colleagues at the Institute for Defense Analyses in Princeton worked out the mathematics of hidden Markov models. In 1967 Andrew Viterbi published his algorithm for decoding signals sent over noisy channels. In 1971 the US agency ARPA began a five-year programme of speech understanding research. In 1975 James Baker at Carnegie Mellon described his Dragon recogniser, and Frederick Jelinek's group at IBM published its own statistical approach. In 1982 James and Janet Baker founded Dragon Systems in Massachusetts.`,facts:[`ARPA's speech understanding programme ran for five years from 1971.`,`Dragon Systems was founded by James and Janet Baker in 1982.`]},"tech:bayesnets":{intro:`A Bayesian network represents uncertain knowledge as a diagram of causes and effects, each link carrying probabilities: flu makes fever likely, winter makes flu more likely. Given what is observed, such as a fever in January, it calculates how likely every unobserved cause is, using the laws of probability rather than improvised rules.`,how:`Each node is a variable, such as a disease, a symptom or a test result, and arrows run from causes to effects. Each node stores a table of how likely its values are given its parents. Because each variable depends directly on only a few others, the sums stay manageable. When evidence arrives, messages pass along the links, updating every belief in step with Bayes' rule.`,history:`In 1982 Judea Pearl at the University of California, Los Angeles, showed how beliefs could be updated by passing messages along a tree of causes. In 1985 Pearl coined the name Bayesian network. In 1988 his book Probabilistic Reasoning in Intelligent Systems gave the field its foundations. In 1989 the ALARM network, built to monitor patients under anaesthesia, became a standard test case. In 1990 David Heckerman's Pathfinder used a Bayesian network to help pathologists diagnose diseases of the lymph nodes. In 1997 Microsoft Office's animated assistant drew on Bayesian research by Eric Horvitz's team.`,facts:[`The ALARM monitoring network linked 37 variables (1989).`,`Pathfinder covered more than 60 diseases of the lymph nodes (1990).`]},"tech:trees":{intro:`A decision tree learner builds its own rules from examples. Given thousands of solved cases, each described by attributes and labelled with the right answer, it finds the single question that best splits the cases into purer groups, then repeats on each group, producing a tree of questions that anyone can read and check.`,how:`At each step the program tries every attribute, such as whether the outlook is sunny or whether income is above some level, and measures how much each would reduce the mix of answers, using information gain from information theory or a similar score. It splits on the best and carries on until the groups are pure or too small. Pruning then cuts off branches that fit quirks of the training cases rather than real patterns.`,history:`In 1966 Earl Hunt and colleagues described the Concept Learning System, which built trees of questions from examples. In 1979 Ross Quinlan in Sydney described ID3 and used it to classify chess endgame positions. In 1984 Leo Breiman, Jerome Friedman, Richard Olshen and Charles Stone published Classification and Regression Trees. In 1986 Quinlan's description of ID3 appeared in the first volume of the new journal Machine Learning. In 1993 Quinlan released C4.5, which handled numbers and missing values and pruned its trees.`,facts:[`Machine Learning, the journal, began publishing in 1986.`,`Quinlan's C4.5 program and book appeared in 1993.`]},"tech:tdlearning":{intro:`Temporal-difference learning teaches a program to predict outcomes, such as who will win a game, by comparing each prediction with the next one rather than waiting for the final result. Each guess is nudged towards the slightly better-informed guess that follows it, so lessons about which positions matter flow backwards through the game.`,how:`The program keeps an evaluation of each situation, often a neural network. After each move it compares its old estimate with the new one, plus any reward received, and moves the old one a small step towards it. Over many thousands of games against itself the estimates improve, and choosing moves that lead to the best-rated positions makes a strong player that has learned without a teacher.`,history:`In 1959 Arthur Samuel's checkers program already adjusted its evaluation of positions towards later evaluations, an early form of the idea. In 1983 Andrew Barto, Richard Sutton and Charles Anderson used related methods to teach a simulated cart to balance a pole. In 1988 Sutton set out temporal-difference learning in the journal Machine Learning. In 1989 Chris Watkins's Cambridge thesis introduced Q-learning, which learns the value of each action in each situation. In 1992 Gerald Tesauro at IBM trained TD-Gammon, a backgammon program, by playing it against itself. By 1995 TD-Gammon played close to the best human level, and its unusual opening moves changed how experts played.`,facts:[`Watkins introduced Q-learning in his 1989 PhD thesis.`,`Sutton and Barto's textbook Reinforcement Learning appeared in 1998.`]},"tech:smt":{intro:`Statistical machine translation learns to translate from examples instead of rules. Given millions of sentences with their human translations, it works out by counting which words tend to correspond and which word orders sound natural, then translates new sentences by picking the most probable rendering.`,how:`The approach treats translation like decoding a garbled message. A translation model, learned from pairs of matching sentences, says how likely a French word is to stand for an English one; a language model, learned from plain English, says how likely an English sentence is. Training repeatedly guesses which words line up and refines the probabilities. The decoder then searches for the English sentence that scores best on both.`,history:`In 1988 an IBM speech-recognition team including Peter Brown, Robert Mercer and Frederick Jelinek first presented the idea of translating by statistics. In 1990 the team published its approach in Computational Linguistics, trained on Canadian parliamentary records in English and French. In 1993 the group's paper "The Mathematics of Statistical Machine Translation" set out five models of increasing power, the IBM Models. In 1993 Brown and Mercer left IBM for the hedge fund Renaissance Technologies. In 1999 a summer workshop at Johns Hopkins University released free tools for building such systems.`,facts:[`The 1993 paper described five translation models, IBM Models 1 to 5.`,`IBM's team called its 1990 system Candide.`]},"tech:neuralsteer":{intro:`Neural steering trains a neural network to drive a vehicle by watching a human. A camera image of the road goes in, a steering direction comes out, and the network learns the link from examples of how a person steered, without anyone programming in what a road edge or a lane marking looks like.`,how:`ALVINN shrinks each camera frame to a small grid, feeds it through a hidden layer of a few neurons and outputs one of many steering directions. It is trained by backpropagation while a person drives for a few minutes; to teach it to recover from mistakes, the training images are shifted and rotated to show the road as if the van had drifted. The computer then steers while a person handles the speed.`,history:`In 1986 Carnegie Mellon University's Navlab project began building self-driving vans. In 1987 Ernst Dickmanns's van VaMoRs drove itself on an empty German motorway at up to about 90 km/h. In 1989 Dean Pomerleau's ALVINN, the Autonomous Land Vehicle In a Neural Network, learned to steer a Navlab. In 1995 Pomerleau and Todd Jochem drove Navlab 5 across the US from Pittsburgh to San Diego with the computer steering almost all the way. In 1995 Dickmanns's Mercedes drove itself from Munich to Copenhagen and back on public motorways.`,facts:[`ALVINN's input was a 30 by 32 pixel image (1989).`,`Dickmanns's Mercedes reached about 175 km/h on the autobahn in 1995.`]},"tech:collabfilter":{intro:`Collaborative filtering recommends things by the crowd rather than by their content. If people who rated the same films or articles as you, in the same way, also liked something you have not seen, you will probably like it too. The system needs no understanding of what the items are, only lots of people's ratings.`,how:`Each user is a row of ratings. To predict how much someone will like an item, the system finds other users whose past ratings closely match theirs, the neighbours, and averages what those neighbours gave the item, weighting each by how similar they are. More ratings make better neighbours, while newcomers with few ratings are hard to serve, the cold-start problem.`,history:`In 1992 David Goldberg and colleagues at Xerox PARC built Tapestry, which let people filter email by other readers' reactions, and coined the term collaborative filtering. In 1994 Paul Resnick and colleagues at MIT and the University of Minnesota built GroupLens, which predicted which Usenet news articles readers would like. In 1995 researchers from the MIT Media Lab founded Firefly to sell recommendations of music and films. In 1997 the University of Minnesota opened MovieLens, a film-rating site built for research. In 1998 Microsoft bought Firefly.`,facts:[`MovieLens began collecting film ratings in 1997 and shared them with researchers.`,`Microsoft bought Firefly in 1998.`]},"tech:svm":{intro:`A support vector machine learns to sort examples into two classes by drawing the boundary that leaves the widest possible gap, the margin, between them. A mathematical trick lets it bend that boundary into complex curves without the cost of working in the bent space directly, and its sound theory makes it hard to fool even with few examples.`,how:`Each example is a point described by numbers. Training solves an optimisation problem that finds the dividing line or plane with the largest margin, and only the points closest to it, the support vectors, decide where it goes. A kernel function measures similarity between points as if they had been mapped into a space with many more dimensions, where a flat boundary can separate classes that were tangled. Penalties allow a few points to sit on the wrong side.`,history:`In 1964 Vladimir Vapnik and Alexey Chervonenkis in Moscow described a classifier that separates two classes with the widest margin. In 1990 Vapnik moved to Bell Labs in New Jersey. In 1992 Bernhard Boser, Isabelle Guyon and Vapnik at Bell Labs added the kernel trick. In 1995 Corinna Cortes and Vapnik added the soft margin, which tolerates a few mistakes. In 1998 Thorsten Joachims showed that the method sorted text documents very well, and John Platt at Microsoft Research published a much faster way to train it.`,facts:[`Vapnik's book The Nature of Statistical Learning Theory appeared in 1995.`,`Platt's 1998 training method was called sequential minimal optimisation, SMO.`]},"tech:contspeech":{intro:`Continuous dictation software types what you say as you speak in ordinary flowing sentences, without pausing between words. After a short session reading set text aloud so it can adapt to your voice, it takes dictation on a home PC at a hundred words a minute or more, with errors you correct by voice or keyboard.`,how:`The program runs a hidden Markov model recogniser with a vocabulary of tens of thousands of words and a language model of which words follow which. Because fluent speech has no gaps between words, it searches many possible splits at once, which needs a fast processor. The reading session tunes its sound models to the speaker's voice and microphone, and each correction teaches it a little more.`,history:`In 1990 Dragon Systems released DragonDictate, which needed users to pause briefly after every word. In 1997 Dragon released NaturallySpeaking for Windows PCs, the first continuous dictation program sold to the public, and IBM followed with ViaVoice. In 2000 the Belgian speech company Lernout & Hauspie bought Dragon Systems. In 2000 Lernout & Hauspie collapsed in an accounting scandal and went bankrupt. In 2001 ScanSoft bought the Dragon software.`,facts:[`NaturallySpeaking 1.0 cost about $695 (1997).`,`DragonDictate users had to pause after every word (1990).`]},"tech:ensembles":{intro:`Ensemble methods combine many simple models into one strong one. Boosting trains a series of small decision trees, each concentrating on the cases the earlier ones got wrong; a random forest grows hundreds of trees on random samples of the data and lets them vote. Together they are far more accurate than any single tree.`,how:`In AdaBoost every training case has a weight. After each weak learner is trained, the weights of the cases it got wrong are raised, so the next learner concentrates on them, and the final answer is a weighted vote. A random forest gives each tree a random sample of the cases and, at each split, a random handful of features to choose from, so the trees make different mistakes that cancel out in the vote.`,history:`In 1990 Robert Schapire proved that weak learners, only slightly better than guessing, could be combined into a strong one. In 1995 Yoav Freund and Schapire published AdaBoost. In 1996 Leo Breiman introduced bagging, training many models on random resamples of the data. In 2001 Breiman published random forests, and Paul Viola and Michael Jones used boosting to build a face detector fast enough for live video. In 2001 Jerome Friedman published gradient boosting, which fits each new tree to the errors left by the others. In 2003 Freund and Schapire won the Gödel Prize for AdaBoost.`,facts:[`The Viola-Jones face detector ran at about 15 frames a second in 2001.`,`Freund and Schapire won the 2003 Gödel Prize.`]},"tech:phrasemt":{intro:`Phrase-based translation learns to translate chunks of words, not single words, from millions of translated sentences, so idioms and common word groups come out right. A huge language model, trained on far more text than any person could read, checks that the result reads like fluent writing in the target language.`,how:`Word alignments found in the training sentences are used to collect every pair of phrases that line up consistently, such as "il y a" with "there is", each with probabilities. To translate, a decoder breaks the sentence into phrases, tries their possible translations and orders, and scores each candidate with the phrase table, a penalty for reordering and a language model, keeping only the best partial translations as it goes.`,history:`In 2002 Kishore Papineni and colleagues at IBM published BLEU, an automatic score that compares machine output with human translations, making progress easy to measure. In 2003 Philipp Koehn, Franz Och and Daniel Marcu showed that translating phrases beat translating words. In 2004 Och joined Google to lead its translation work. In 2006 Google launched its own statistical translation service. In 2007 Koehn and colleagues released Moses, a free phrase-based translation system.`,facts:[`The BLEU translation score was published in 2002.`,`Moses, a free phrase-based translator, was released in 2007.`]},"tech:matrixfact":{intro:`Matrix factorisation for recommendations squeezes a giant table of who rated what into a few hidden taste numbers for each person and each item. A film might score high on action and low on romance, and a viewer the same; multiplying the two predicts how much the viewer will like the film, even if nobody quite like them has rated it.`,how:`The ratings table is mostly empty, since each person rates only a few films. The method gives every user and every film a short list of numbers, perhaps fifty, and adjusts them step by step until their products match the known ratings as closely as possible, with a penalty that stops it memorising noise. The hidden factors are not named in advance; they emerge from the data and often turn out to mean something.`,history:`In 2006 Netflix launched the Netflix Prize, offering $1 million to anyone who could beat its own recommender's accuracy by 10 per cent, and released 100 million ratings. In 2006 Simon Funk, a lone programmer, posted on his blog a simple way to factorise the ratings that jumped him up the leaderboard. In 2007 the BellKor team from AT&T Labs won the first $50,000 Progress Prize. In 2009 BellKor's Pragmatic Chaos won the grand prize with a blend of hundreds of models. In 2010 Netflix cancelled a planned second contest over privacy worries.`,facts:[`The Netflix Prize data held about 100 million ratings of 17,770 films (2006).`,`The winners submitted 20 minutes before a rival team with the same score (2009).`]},"tech:robocar":{intro:`Probabilistic robot cars drive themselves using lasers, radar, cameras, maps and satellite positioning, and treat every reading as uncertain. Instead of trusting any one sensor, the car keeps a running estimate of where it is and what lies ahead, with error bars, and plans a path that is safe given what it does not know.`,how:`Laser range finders on the roof sweep the ground ahead many times a second. Probabilistic filters merge these readings with satellite positioning and the car's motion sensors to estimate its position and build a map of drivable ground, marking each patch as safe, blocked or unknown. Stanford's car learned from recordings of human driving which laser patterns meant real obstacles, and used a camera to see further ahead so it could drive faster.`,history:`In 2004 the US military research agency DARPA held its first Grand Challenge, a race for driverless vehicles across the Mojave Desert, and no entrant got further than about 7 miles. In 2005 Stanford's Stanley, led by Sebastian Thrun, won the second Grand Challenge, one of five vehicles to finish. In 2005 Thrun, Wolfram Burgard and Dieter Fox published the textbook Probabilistic Robotics. In 2007 Carnegie Mellon's Boss won DARPA's Urban Challenge on a former air base in California, obeying traffic rules among other cars. In 2009 Google began a self-driving car project led by Thrun.`,facts:[`No car went further than about 7 miles in the 2004 Grand Challenge.`,`Stanley won a $2 million prize (2005).`]},"tech:dnnasr":{intro:`Deep speech recognition replaces the statistical sound models inside a speech recogniser with a deep neural network. The network looks at a short window of sound and estimates which of thousands of speech-sound states it belongs to, far more accurately than the older models, so the recogniser makes roughly a third fewer mistakes on everyday speech.`,how:`The recogniser keeps its hidden Markov model structure and its language model, but the probabilities for each state now come from a network with several hidden layers, trained on hundreds of hours of transcribed speech. It looks at a window of neighbouring sound frames, and its outputs are the thousands of context-dependent sound states, called senones, of the older system. Graphics cards make training on so much data practical.`,history:`In 2009 Abdel-rahman Mohamed, George Dahl and Geoffrey Hinton in Toronto showed deep networks beating older models on a standard small speech test. In 2010 Dahl, working with Microsoft Research, showed the gain held on a larger task. In 2011 Frank Seide, Gang Li and Dong Yu at Microsoft cut errors sharply on recordings of telephone conversations. In 2012 researchers from Toronto, Microsoft, Google and IBM published a joint paper agreeing that deep networks were better. In 2012 Google switched Android voice search to a deep network, and Microsoft showed live English speech translated into spoken Chinese.`,facts:[`Microsoft's 2011 system was trained on about 300 hours of telephone speech.`,`The joint paper of 2012 came from four research groups.`]},"tech:deepqa":{intro:`DeepQA is a question-answering system that reads a question in ordinary English, searches millions of documents for possible answers and judges how confident it is in each. It does not look up one fact in a table; it gathers evidence from many sources with many small scoring programs, and answers only when its confidence is high enough.`,how:`The question is parsed to find what kind of thing is wanted: a city, a person, a year. Searches produce hundreds of candidate answers from encyclopaedias, dictionaries, news stories and literature held in memory. Hundreds of separate scorers check each candidate against types, dates, places and the wording of passages, and a machine-learned model combines their scores into one confidence. It runs on thousands of processor cores so that it can answer in about three seconds.`,history:`In 2007 IBM researchers led by David Ferrucci began building Watson to compete on the US television quiz Jeopardy. In 2010 Ferrucci's team described the DeepQA design in AI Magazine. In 2011 Watson beat the champions Ken Jennings and Brad Rutter over two games broadcast in February. In 2011 Jennings wrote beneath his final answer that he welcomed "our new computer overlords". In 2013 IBM began offering Watson to hospitals as an adviser on cancer treatment.`,facts:[`Watson ran on 90 IBM Power 750 servers with 2,880 processor cores (2011).`,`Watson held about 200 million pages of content in memory (2011).`,`IBM gave Watson's $1 million prize to charity (2011).`]},"tech:chesschips":{intro:`Search chips put the heart of a chess program, generating moves and judging positions, into custom silicon. A single chip can examine hundreds of thousands of positions a second, far beyond a program on a general-purpose computer, and many such chips working together let a machine look deep enough to beat grandmasters.`,how:`The move generator is an array of small circuits, one for each square, that find all legal moves at once and offer the most promising first, which makes alpha-beta pruning work well. Judging a position, counting material and simple features, is also done in hardware. A host computer splits the search tree among the chips and runs the overall search, while the chips search the last few moves of each line.`,history:`In 1980 Ken Thompson and Joe Condon at Bell Labs built Belle, a chess machine with special move-generating hardware, which won the World Computer Chess Championship. In 1983 Belle became the first machine to earn a US master rating. In 1985 Feng-hsiung Hsu began designing a single-chip move generator at Carnegie Mellon University, the basis of the machines ChipTest and Deep Thought. In 1989 Garry Kasparov beat Deep Thought easily in a two-game match. In 1997 IBM's Deep Blue, Deep Thought's successor, beat Kasparov in a six-game match in New York.`,facts:[`Deep Blue won its 1997 match against Kasparov by 3.5 points to 2.5.`,`Belle was the 1980 World Computer Chess Champion.`]},"tech:gpgpu":{intro:`GPU computing uses the graphics card, built to shade millions of pixels at once, as a general-purpose parallel processor for any heavy calculation: physics, finance, medical scans, codebreaking. CUDA lets programmers write ordinary C code for it, instead of disguising their maths as drawing operations.`,how:`A graphics chip has more than a hundred simple cores grouped into blocks that run the same instruction on different data together. With CUDA the programmer writes a kernel, a function that thousands of threads run at once, each on its own piece of the data, and copies the data into the card's fast memory. Problems that split into many independent pieces can run tens of times faster than on the main processor.`,history:`In 2002 Mark Harris coined the term GPGPU for tricking graphics cards into doing general sums by disguising them as pixel shading. In 2004 Ian Buck and colleagues at Stanford published Brook, a language for programming graphics cards for general work. In 2006 NVIDIA launched the GeForce 8800, its first chip designed for CUDA, with Buck leading the software. In 2008 the Khronos Group published OpenCL 1.0, an open rival to CUDA proposed by Apple. In 2010 China's Tianhe-1A, built with NVIDIA graphics processors, became the world's fastest supercomputer.`,facts:[`The GeForce 8800 GTX had 128 stream processors (2006).`,`Tianhe-1A used about 7,000 NVIDIA graphics processors (2010).`]},"tech:voiceassist":{intro:`A voice assistant answers spoken requests on a phone: set an alarm, send a message, find a restaurant, tell me the weather. It turns speech into text, works out what the person wants, carries it out using the phone's apps and web services, and replies in a synthetic voice, sometimes with a joke.`,how:`The phone records the request and sends it to the company's servers, where speech recognition turns it into words. Language understanding maps the words onto a task and its details, such as a reminder, a time and a place, using rules and statistics tuned on many examples. The assistant then calls the right service, the calendar, a maps database or a weather feed, and the answer is spoken back and shown on the screen.`,history:`In 2003 the US military research agency DARPA funded CALO, a five-year project led by SRI International to build a learning personal assistant. In 2007 Dag Kittlaus, Adam Cheyer and Tom Gruber founded Siri as an SRI spin-off to build an assistant for consumers. In 2010 Siri launched as an iPhone app in February, and Apple bought the company that April. In 2011 Siri was built into the iPhone 4S. In 2012 Google launched Google Now for Android phones. In 2014 Microsoft launched Cortana, and Amazon its Echo speaker with the Alexa assistant.`,facts:[`CALO brought together about 300 researchers from 25 organisations (2003).`,`Siri on the iPhone 4S understood English, French and German at launch (2011).`]},"tech:sampling":{intro:`A digital sampler records a real sound, a violin note, a choir, a dog's bark or a slammed door, as a stream of numbers, then plays it back from a musical keyboard at any pitch. Musicians can play any recorded sound as an instrument, instead of building sounds from electronic oscillators.`,how:`An analogue-to-digital converter measures the sound thousands of times a second, and each measurement is stored in memory as a number. To play a higher note the sampler reads the numbers out faster, and to play a lower one slower, so one recording covers a range of keys. Memory is tiny and expensive, so samples last only a second or two, and a loop repeats part of a note to make it sustain.`,history:`In 1979 Peter Vogel and Kim Ryrie in Sydney launched the Fairlight CMI, a computer musical instrument with a screen and a light pen for drawing and editing sounds. In 1980 Peter Gabriel used a Fairlight on his third solo album, and other well-off pop stars soon followed. In 1981 E-mu Systems in California released the Emulator, a sampling keyboard at a fraction of the Fairlight's price. In 1984 Ensoniq's Mirage brought sampling within reach of ordinary musicians. In 1988 Akai released the MPC60, designed with Roger Linn, a sampling drum machine with pads that hip hop producers took up.`,facts:[`The first Fairlight CMI stored sounds as 8-bit numbers (1979).`,`The Ensoniq Mirage cost about $1,700 (1984).`]},"tech:fmsynth":{intro:`FM synthesis makes rich sounds by using one tone to wobble the pitch of another very quickly. Instead of filtering a buzzing waveform, as analogue synthesizers do, it creates complex harmonics from a few simple sine waves, and does it cheaply in digital chips: bright electric pianos, bells, brass and basses.`,how:`An oscillator called the carrier makes the tone you hear, while a second, the modulator, varies the carrier's frequency at audio speed. The ratio between their frequencies sets which harmonics appear, and the modulator's strength sets how bright the sound is, so changing it during a note makes it bloom and fade like a struck string. The Yamaha DX7 links six such operators in 32 arrangements called algorithms.`,history:`In 1967 John Chowning at Stanford University found that modulating one tone by another at audio speed produced rich, natural-sounding timbres. In 1973 he published the method in the Journal of the Audio Engineering Society. In 1977 Stanford's US patent on the method was granted, with Yamaha as its licensee. In 1983 Yamaha launched the DX7, the first big-selling digital synthesizer. In 1987 Yamaha's FM chips reached home computers through the AdLib sound card for PCs. In 1988 Sega's Mega Drive console used a Yamaha FM chip for its music.`,facts:[`The DX7 cost about $2,000 at launch (1983).`,`The DX7 had six operators and 32 algorithms (1983).`]},"tech:midi":{intro:`MIDI, the Musical Instrument Digital Interface, is a standard way for electronic instruments and computers to talk to each other. It carries no sound, only instructions: which note to play, how hard, when to let go, which sound to use. Any keyboard can play any other maker's synthesizer, and a computer can play them all.`,how:`Instruments are linked by cables with round five-pin plugs, carrying a serial signal at 31,250 bits per second. Messages are only a few bytes long: note on, with a note number and how hard the key was struck; note off; a change of sound; the movement of a pitch wheel. Sixteen channels share one cable, so a single line can play sixteen different instruments, each listening to its own channel.`,history:`In 1981 Dave Smith and Chet Wood of Sequential Circuits proposed a Universal Synthesizer Interface at the Audio Engineering Society convention in New York. In 1983 a Sequential Prophet-600 and a Roland Jupiter-6 played each other over MIDI at the NAMM music trade show in California. In 1983 the MIDI 1.0 specification was published, agreed between American and Japanese makers. In 1985 the Atari ST computer went on sale with MIDI sockets built in. In 1991 General MIDI fixed a standard list of 128 sounds, so a song file played with the right instruments on any compatible device. In 2013 Smith and Roland's founder Ikutaro Kakehashi received a Technical Grammy Award for MIDI.`,facts:[`MIDI carried 31,250 bits per second over one cable (1983).`,`General MIDI (1991) defined 128 standard instrument sounds.`]},"tech:sequencer":{intro:`A software sequencer turns a personal computer into a multitrack recorder for MIDI. A musician plays a part on a keyboard and the computer stores every note; wrong notes can be fixed on the screen, timing tidied and choruses copied, and the whole arrangement played back on a rack of synthesizers at once, with no tape.`,how:`Each track records MIDI messages with their times. The program shows the notes as lists or as bars on a grid, and edits are just changes to numbers: move a note, change its length or loudness. Quantising snaps sloppy timing to the nearest beat. On playback the computer sends each track's notes to the right instrument channel, and the tempo can change without altering the pitch.`,history:`In 1984 Karl Steinberg and Manfred Rürup in Hamburg released Pro-16, a 16-track sequencer for the Commodore 64. In 1985 Mark of the Unicorn released Performer for the Apple Macintosh. In 1985 the Atari ST's built-in MIDI ports made it the cheapest serious sequencing computer, and it became a fixture of European studios. In 1987 C-Lab in Hamburg released Creator, written by Gerhard Lengeling and Chris Adam. In 1989 Steinberg released Cubase for the Atari ST, showing each part as a block on a timeline that could be dragged around.`,facts:[`Pro-16 recorded 16 tracks of MIDI on a Commodore 64 (1984).`,`The Atari 520ST sold for about $800 with a monitor (1985).`]},"tech:daw":{intro:`A digital audio workstation records real sound, voices and instruments, straight onto a computer's hard disc and lets the engineer edit it on screen the way a word processor edits text. Takes can be cut, copied, moved and undone without the razor blades and splicing tape of the old studio, and MIDI and audio sit side by side.`,how:`Sound comes in through converters on a special card and is stored on disc as digital audio, 44,100 measurements a second for CD quality. The screen shows each track's waveform, and edits only change a playlist of which parts of the files to play and when, so nothing is destroyed. Digital signal processor chips on the card handle the mixing, equalising and effects that the computer's main processor is too slow to do.`,history:`In 1984 Peter Gotcher and Evan Brooks founded the company that became Digidesign, at first selling sound chips for drum machines. In 1989 Digidesign released Sound Tools, a two-track recorder and editor for the Macintosh. In 1991 its Pro Tools recorded four tracks at once on a Macintosh fitted with Digidesign's cards. In 1993 Emagic in Germany released Logic, adding audio to MIDI sequencing. In 1995 Digidesign was bought by Avid, the video editing company. In 1999 Ricky Martin's "Livin' la Vida Loca" became the first US number one recorded, edited and mixed entirely in Pro Tools.`,facts:[`CD audio stores 44,100 samples a second on each of two channels (1982).`,`"Livin' la Vida Loca" topped the US chart in 1999.`]},"tech:vst":{intro:`Audio plug-ins are small programs that load inside a recording program and add an effect or an instrument: a reverb, a compressor, an equaliser, a whole synthesizer. With an open standard, any company can sell plug-ins that work in any host program, and the computer's own processor does the work instead of costly studio hardware.`,how:`The host passes the plug-in blocks of audio samples, a few hundred at a time, and the plug-in hands them back processed; an instrument plug-in receives MIDI notes and returns sound. Each plug-in draws its own control panel, often copying the knobs of classic hardware. Because the code runs on the main processor, a faster computer means more plug-ins at once, and a free developer kit lets small firms and hobbyists join in.`,history:`In 1996 Steinberg released Cubase VST, short for Virtual Studio Technology, which mixed and processed audio on the computer's own processor and opened its plug-in format to other companies. In 1996 Native Instruments was founded in Berlin to make software instruments. In 1999 VST 2.0 let plug-ins act as instruments played by MIDI, and Steinberg's Neon was among the first. In 2001 Apple introduced its own plug-in format, Audio Units, for Mac OS X. In 2008 Steinberg released VST 3.`,facts:[`Cubase VST ran its effects on a Power Macintosh with no extra DSP hardware (1996).`,`Steinberg released VST 3 in 2008.`]},"tech:autotune":{intro:`Pitch correction measures the note a singer or player is producing many times a second and gently shifts it to the nearest correct note of the chosen scale. Used lightly it rescues a good take with a few sour notes; turned up to act instantly, it snaps the voice from note to note with an unnatural, robotic sound.`,how:`The program finds the pitch of the incoming sound by autocorrelation, comparing the waveform with delayed copies of itself to see how often it repeats. It compares that pitch with the target scale and resamples the sound slightly faster or slower to move it, without changing the timing. A retune speed setting decides how quickly it pulls the note: slow for natural slides, zero for a hard, stepped effect.`,history:`In 1997 Andy Hildebrand, who had spent years interpreting seismic data for the oil industry with the same mathematics, released Auto-Tune through his company Antares as a plug-in for Pro Tools. In 1998 Cher's song "Believe" used Auto-Tune with the retune speed at zero, and the stepped sound became known as the Cher effect. In 2005 the singer T-Pain made the hard Auto-Tune sound his trademark. In 2008 Kanye West used it on almost every song of his album 808s & Heartbreak.`,facts:[`"Believe" topped the UK singles chart for seven weeks (1998).`,`Auto-Tune's fastest retune speed, zero, produced the Cher effect (1998).`]},"tech:loopdaw":{intro:`Loop-based production builds music from short repeating clips of audio and MIDI arranged on a grid. Clips stretch automatically to the song's tempo, so loops recorded at different speeds fit together, and the musician can launch, swap and change them while the music plays, which makes the computer an instrument for the stage as well as the studio.`,how:`Each clip is analysed to find its beats, so the program can time-stretch it, speeding it up or slowing it down without changing its pitch. Clips sit in a grid of slots, and triggering one starts it on the next bar, in time with everything else. Effects, filters and instruments can be changed live from a MIDI controller, and a performance can be recorded as an arrangement and edited later.`,history:`In 1997 the Belgian firm Image-Line released FruityLoops, written by Didier Dambrin, as a simple drum-pattern program. In 1998 Sonic Foundry's Acid matched loops to a song's tempo and key automatically. In 1999 Gerhard Behles, Robert Henke and Bernd Roggendorf founded Ableton in Berlin. In 2001 Ableton released Live 1.0, built for performing on stage as much as recording. In 2004 Apple's GarageBand brought loop-based recording to every new Mac for free. In 2009 Ableton and Akai launched the APC40, a controller made for playing Live.`,facts:[`Sonic Foundry's Acid matched loops to tempo automatically in 1998.`,`GarageBand came free with new Macs from 2004.`]},"tech:iterative":{intro:`Iterative and incremental development builds a large system in a series of small working versions instead of in one go. Each round adds a slice of features to a system that already runs, and is designed, coded and tested before the next begins, so problems surface early and the customer sees real progress and can change their mind.`,how:`The project is split into increments, each a few weeks or months long, ordered so that the riskiest or most important parts come first. Every increment ends with a working, tested system that users can try, and what they learn feeds the plan for the next. Instead of one huge integration at the end, pieces join a system that is already integrated and tested, one at a time.`,history:`In 1970 Winston Royce's paper on managing large software projects, with its famous diagram of stages flowing downhill, in fact advised building a pilot version first. In 1972 IBM's Federal Systems Division built the command system for the US Navy's Trident submarines in four iterations of about six months each. In 1975 Victor Basili and Albert Turner described iterative enhancement in IEEE Transactions on Software Engineering. In 1986 Barry Boehm described the spiral model, in which each loop is an iteration chosen to reduce the biggest remaining risk. In 1988 Tom Gilb's book Principles of Software Engineering Management argued for evolutionary delivery in small steps. In 2003 Craig Larman and Victor Basili published a history of the approach in IEEE Computer.`,facts:[`Royce's 1970 paper recommended doing the job twice, the first time as a pilot.`,`Larman and Basili's 2003 history traced iterative methods back to the 1950s.`]},"tech:vmodel":{intro:`The V-model draws a software project as a letter V. Down the left side the work moves from the customer's requirements to the system design, the design of each part and the code at the bottom; up the right side each level is checked by its own tests, from single units to the complete system measured against what the customer asked for.`,how:`Every stage on the left produces a document and, at the same time, the plan for the test that will check it on the right: requirements are matched by acceptance tests, the architecture by integration tests, each module's design by unit tests. Verification asks whether each step was built right, validation whether the whole is the right thing. Traceability links make sure every requirement is designed, coded and tested.`,history:`In 1979 Barry Boehm of the defence firm TRW published a paper on verifying and validating software requirements and designs that drew the stages and their matching checks as a V. In 1992 the German government published V-Modell 92, making the approach the rule for its own software projects. In 1992 the aviation standard DO-178B required that the most critical flight software be traced from requirement to test and tested down to the individual decisions in the code. In 1998 IEC 61508 brought the same discipline to safety systems in industry. In 2011 ISO 26262 adapted it for the electronics of road cars.`,facts:[`IEC 61508 defined four safety integrity levels, SIL 1 to SIL 4 (1998).`,`ISO 26262 defined four automotive safety levels, ASIL A to D (2011).`]},"tech:rad":{intro:`Rapid application development builds business software fast by putting users and programmers in the same room, building a rough working version in weeks with high-level tools, showing it, fixing it and repeating. A fixed deadline, the timebox, is sacred: if time runs short, features are dropped rather than the date moved.`,how:`Workshops with users replace long written specifications. Small teams use fourth-generation languages, screen painters and visual builders that turn forms and database tables into working screens with little code, so a prototype can change while the users watch. Each timebox lasts weeks or a few months, and early versions are expected to be reworked or thrown away.`,history:`In the mid-1980s Scott Shultz at the chemicals company DuPont ran software projects in fixed timeboxes with users on the team. In 1991 James Martin's book Rapid Application Development gave the approach its name. In 1991 Microsoft released Visual Basic, which let programmers draw windows and buttons and attach code to them. In 1994 sixteen RAD practitioners met in Britain and formed the DSDM Consortium to agree a common method. In 1995 Borland released Delphi, a visual builder that compiled to fast Windows programs.`,facts:[`Visual Basic 1.0 was released in May 1991.`,`The first version of the DSDM method was published in 1995.`]},"tech:cmm":{intro:`A process maturity model is a yardstick for how well a software organisation works, from chaotic to disciplined. Assessors check whether projects are planned, tracked, reviewed, measured and improved, and grade the organisation on a five-level scale. Big customers, starting with the US military, begin asking suppliers for a good grade before signing contracts.`,how:`The Capability Maturity Model has five levels: initial, where success depends on heroes; repeatable, with basic project management; defined, with one standard process across the organisation; managed, with measurement; and optimising, with constant improvement. Each level lists key process areas, such as managing requirements or software versions, and the practices expected in each. ISO 9001 certifies a company's quality management against a shorter list of requirements, in any industry.`,history:`In 1984 the US Department of Defense set up the Software Engineering Institute at Carnegie Mellon University in Pittsburgh. In 1987 the SEI released its first maturity framework, led by Watts Humphrey, a former IBM manager, and the same year ISO 9001 was published. In 1989 Humphrey's book Managing the Software Process explained the levels in detail. In 1991 the SEI published version 1.0 of the Capability Maturity Model. By 2003 most of the world's Level 5 organisations were Indian, and outsourcing firms such as Infosys and Wipro made top grades a selling point. In 2002 the SEI replaced the CMM with the broader CMMI.`,facts:[`The Software Engineering Institute was founded in 1984.`,`The CMM's five levels were set out in version 1.0 in 1991.`]},"tech:kanban":{intro:`Kanban for software shows every piece of work as a card on a board with columns such as to do, doing and done, and sets a strict limit on how many cards may sit in each column at once. New work is pulled in only when something finishes, so work flows steadily instead of piling up half-done.`,how:`The board makes the whole process visible, from request to release. Work-in-progress limits stop people starting more than they can finish; when a column is full, the team helps clear the blockage instead of starting something new. There are no fixed sprints. The team measures lead time, how long a card takes from start to finish, and improves the process step by step, an idea borrowed from Toyota's factories.`,history:`In 1978 Taiichi Ohno's book Toyota Production System described how cards called kanban told each workstation when to make more parts. In 2003 Mary and Tom Poppendieck published Lean Software Development, applying Toyota's ideas to programming. In 2004 David Anderson helped a Microsoft maintenance team cut its lead times by limiting the work in progress. In 2006 at Corbis, a picture agency in Seattle, he ran software work on a card wall that became the model for kanban boards. In 2007 he presented the method at the Agile 2007 conference. In 2010 Anderson's book Kanban set the method out in full.`,facts:[`Ohno's book Toyota Production System appeared in Japanese in 1978.`,`Anderson's 2010 book was subtitled Successful Evolutionary Change for Your Technology Business.`]},"tech:rapidrelease":{intro:`Release trains ship new versions of a program on a fixed timetable, every few weeks, with whatever is ready. A feature that is not finished does not delay the release; it waits for the next train. Automatic updates install each version quietly in the background, so almost all users are on the latest one.`,how:`Code moves through a series of channels: nightly builds for developers, then beta versions for keen users, then the stable release, each step lasting one cycle. Unfinished features stay hidden behind switches, called flags, until they are ready. The program checks for updates by itself and downloads small patches, while automated tests and crash reports catch problems before most people see them.`,history:`In 2008 Google released its Chrome browser with a silent updater that kept users current without asking them. In 2010 the Chrome team moved to a new stable release every six weeks. In 2011 Mozilla adopted a similar cycle, releasing Firefox 4 in March and Firefox 9 in December. In 2012 Mozilla began extended-support releases for companies that wanted slower change. In 2017 Facebook moved its website to continuous deployment, pushing new code out many times a day. In 2021 Chrome shortened its cycle to four weeks.`,facts:[`Chrome reached version 10 in March 2011, two and a half years after launch.`,`Mozilla shipped six major Firefox versions, 4 to 9, in 2011.`]},"tech:scaledagile":{intro:`Scaled agile frameworks try to run hundreds of programmers the agile way, although agile methods were made for single teams of about seven. They group a dozen or so teams into one larger unit, sometimes called a release train, that plans together every couple of months and delivers on a shared rhythm, with extra roles to coordinate the teams.`,how:`In SAFe the teams of a release train meet for a two-day planning event every eight to twelve weeks, agree objectives and map the dependencies between teams. Portfolio levels above decide which large initiatives get money. LeSS keeps things leaner: up to eight teams share one product owner and one list of work, with as few extra roles as possible. Critics say heavy frameworks bring back the bureaucracy that agile set out to remove.`,history:`In 2007 Dean Leffingwell published Scaling Software Agility, drawing on large agile projects. In 2008 Craig Larman and Bas Vodde published Scaling Lean & Agile Development, the first of their books on Large-Scale Scrum, LeSS. In 2011 Leffingwell launched the Scaled Agile Framework, SAFe. In 2012 Henrik Kniberg and Anders Ivarsson described how the music service Spotify organised its engineers into squads, tribes, chapters and guilds. In 2020 the State of Agile survey found SAFe the most widely used scaling framework.`,facts:[`Spotify's 2012 tribes were kept below about 100 people each.`,`Leffingwell's Scaling Software Agility appeared in 2007.`]}};export{e as PEDIA_BUSINESS};